ve access plan a “virtual field trip”\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to observe images and videos of animals that are at the zoo.\nStep 2: Guide learners to observing animals in their habitats to discover their behavior, appearance, their\ndiversity etc.\nStep 3: Learners to take variety of", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "49bf54a72af1b0b0", "path": "lesson-plans/General/LESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES (3).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "General", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 7098, "text": "nload Care for the environment a href https drive google com uc export\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for plants- Observing flower beds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n observe flower beds\n point out which flower beds are watered\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How do we know when flower beds have been watered?\n How do flowers look like when they have not been watered?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs displaying flower beds\nSample flower beds at school\nPencils, crayons, books for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.81-82\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to take a walk around the school as they observe flower beds available\nStep 2: Guide learners to determine which flowerbeds were not watered.\nStep 3: Guide learners to look at the picture of a flowerbed in their books Guide them to appreciate the flowers that\nhave been watered\nStep 4: Guide learners to look at a picture and appreciate that the flowerbed looks fresh and beautiful because it has\nbeen watered.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe flowerbeds during the day at different times.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for plants- Reasons for watering flowerbeds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n observe flower beds\n point out which flower beds are watered\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How do we know when flower beds have been watered?\n How do flowers look like when they have not been watered?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs displaying flower beds\nSample flower beds at school\nPencils, crayons, books for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.83-84\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nGreet learners and ask them to sing the song describing flowers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Show learner’s variety of flowers that have different fragrances in the school compound.\nStep 2: Explain to the learners that the plants will wilt and die quickly if they are not watered\nStep 3: Guide learners to list various reasons for watering flower beds learnt\nStep 4: Learners to discuss the experiences on what time of the day plants should be watered.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe flowerbeds during the day at different times.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for plants- Equipment for watering flowerbeds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n observe flower beds\n point out which flower beds are watered\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How do we know when flower beds have been watered?\n How do flowers look like when they have not been watered?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs displaying flower beds\nSample flower beds at school\nPencils, crayons, books for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.84\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nGreet learners and ask them to sing the song describing flowers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to watch a video showing various equipment that can be used to water plants.\nStep 2: Guide learners to improvise things that can be used to water flowerbeds in school\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss and list down equipment used for watering flowerbeds\nStep 4: Learners to discuss the experiences on what time of the day plants should be watered.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to be given portions of flowerbeds to water them using the equipment improvised.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe flowerbeds during the day at different times.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for plants- Procedure for watering flower beds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n observe flower beds\n point out which flower beds are watered\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How do we know when flower beds have been watered?\n How do flowers look like when they have not been watered?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs displaying flower beds\nSample flower beds at school\nPencils, crayons, books for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.85\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nGreet learners and ask them to sing the song describing flowers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to move out with improvised bottles filled with water and water flower beds\nStep 2: Guide learners to watch a video of other learners watering flowers for enjoyment\nStep 3: Learners to be allocated a portion of flower beds to practice watering them.\nStep 4: Appoint three learners who will act as watering police to water plants daily.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to be given portions of flowerbeds to water them using the equipment improvised.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe flowerbeds during the day at different times.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for plants- Procedure for watering flower beds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n observe flower beds\n point out which flower beds are watered\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How do we know when flower beds have been watered?\n How do flowers look like when they have not been watered?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs displaying flower beds\nSample flower beds at school\nPencils, crayons, books for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.85\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nGreet learners and ask them to sing the song describing flowers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to move out with improvised bottles filled with water and water flower beds\nStep 2: Guide learners to watch a video of other learners watering flowers for enjoyment\nStep 3: Learners to be allocated a portion of flower beds to practice watering them.\nStep 4: Appoint three learners who will act as watering police to water plants daily.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to be given portions of flowerbeds to water them using the equipment improvised.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe flowerbeds during the day at different times.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for animals- Animal feeds\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify common feeds for various domestic animals.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do domestic animals eat?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nCitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nA video clip showing animals feeding\nPhotographs showing different types of animals kept at home\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.86-87\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: prepare a trip to the farm and let learners visit animal farms where there are animals and animal feeds.\nStep 2: Guide learners to share and discuss different experiences about animal feeds.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss what each animal in the pictures is eating on page 40 of their book.\nStep 4: Learners to read the sentence under each picture after you and let them discuss other animal feed they know.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and ask questions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk to their parents/guardians on animal feeds.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for animals- Equipment for watering and feeding animals\nat home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify common feeds for various domestic animals.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do domestic animals eat?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nCitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nA video clip showing animals feeding\nPhotographs showing different types of animals kept at home\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.88\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to visit animal farms where they can find equipment for feeding and watering animals at home.\nStep 2: Guide learners to share and discuss different experiences about equipment for feeding and watering animals at\nhome.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss what each animal in the pictures is eating on page 41 of their book.\nStep 4: Guide learners to visit a farm and observe more about equipment used to feed and water animals.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and ask questions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk to their parents/guardians on equipment for feeding and watering animals at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Le