ness\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to the picture on page 45  and discuss other ways of disposing waste.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to draw and colour different types of waste in their classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Managing waste responsibly- Importance of a clean classroom\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n Identify types of waste in the classroom\n Safely sort out waste in the classroom for appropriate disposal\n Dispose of waste responsibly to limit risk to self, others and environment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What types of waste are found in the classroom?\n How can we dispose of classroom waste responsibly?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nCitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSamples of type of waste\nChart illustrating different types of waste\nPencils, crayons and drawing books\nPictures of common things used in handling waste e.g. broom, dust pan, gloves\nSlogan on recycling\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.98\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize the learners into groups of five\nStep 2: Guide learners to discuss the importance of waste disposal and cleanliness in the classroom.\nStep 3: Guide learner to design a few slogans on recycling and cleanliness to be placed around the school as a way of\ncreating awareness\nStep 4: Guide learners to chant the above slogans and learn any vocabulary therein\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about cleanliness\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to the picture on page 45 and discuss other ways of disposing waste.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to draw and colour different types of waste in their classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Managing waste responsibly- Types of waste in the home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n Identify types of waste in the classroom\n Safely sort out waste in the classroom for appropriate disposal\n Dispose of waste responsibly to limit risk to self, others and environment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What types of waste are found in the classroom?\n How can we dispose of classroom waste responsibly?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nCitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSamples of type of waste\nChart illustrating different types of waste\nPencils, crayons and drawing books\nPictures of common things used in handling waste e.g. broom, dust pan, gloves\nSlogan on recycling\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.99\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Introduce the term recycling and guide learners to understand it.\nStep 2: Guide learners to collect waste around the home and list them down on their books.\nStep 3: Guide learner to discuss their findings\nStep 4: Guide learners to draw a picture of a house and from their presentation above, colour the areas where the\nwaste is found in their homes.\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about cleanliness\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to watch a video illustrating the different waste materials that are found in the home environment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to draw and colour different types of waste in their classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Managing waste responsibly- Sorting waste for safe disposal at\nhome\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n Identify types of waste in the home\n Safely sort out waste in the classroom for appropriate disposal\n Dispose of waste responsibly to limit risk to self, others and environment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What types of waste are found in the classroom?\n How can we dispose of classroom waste responsibly?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Responsibility\nEnvironmental education\nCitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSamples of type of waste\nChart illustrating different types of waste\nPencils, crayons and drawing books\nPictures of common things used in handling waste e.g. broom, dust pan, gloves\nSlogan on recycling\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.100\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to watch a video showing how waste at home can be sorted and disposed according to type of\nmaterial.\nStep 2: Divide the classroom into different sections and assign students.\nStep 3: Let learners to discuss which waste materials can be found there and how they should be disposed after\nsorting\nStep 4: Learners to discuss among themselves how to handle waste safely at home and how to dispose appropriately\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about cleanliness\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to watch a video illustrating the different waste materials that are found in the home environment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to draw and colour different types of waste in their classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for water- How water is wasted at home and school\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to state ways in which water is wasted at home and school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How is water wasted at home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideo clips showing water wastage\nCharts illustrating different practices that lead to water wastage\nPictures on good and bad ways of using water\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.102-104\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Pair up learners and ask them to identify activities that involve usage of water at home and in school.\nStep 2: Learners to watch a video clip illustrating water wastage. Ask learners to point out the ways water has been\nwasted.\nStep 3: Guide learners to say what they can see in each pictures on page 48 of learner’s book.\nStep 4: Learners to discuss other ways and situations where they can save water\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about water wastage\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to watch a video illustrating the different ways of wasting water\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be taught effects of water wastage with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for water- ways of using water sparingly\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to state ways in which water is wasted at home and school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How is water wasted at home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideo clips showing water wastage\nCharts illustrating different practices that lead to water wastage\nPictures on good and bad ways of using water\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.104-105\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Pair up learners and give them oranges and ask them to go to the tap and wash.\nStep 2: Guide learners through illustration how to wash oranges at the tap.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss ways in which water is used sparingly at home and at school\nStep 4: Guide learners to compose and recite a short poem on the need to conserve water.\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about water wastage\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be taught effects of water wastage with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for water- ways of using water sparingly\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to state ways in which water is wasted at home and school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How is water wasted at home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideo clips showing water wastage\nCharts illustrating different practices that lead to water wastage\nPictures on good and bad ways of using water\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.104-105\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Pair up learners and give them oranges and ask them to go to the tap and wash.\nStep 2: Guide learners through illustration how to wash oranges at the tap.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss ways in which water is used sparingly at home and at school\nStep 4: Guide learners to compose and recite a short poem on the need to conserve water.\nSUMMARY\nLearner to sing a song about water wastage\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be taught effects of water wastage with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for water- Using water sparingly at home and school\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to state ways in which water is wasted at home and school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How is water wasted at home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideo clips showing water wastage\nCharts illustrating different practices that lead to water wastage\nPictures on good and bad ways of using water\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.106\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to share experiences on occasions they were facing water scarcity at home and school\nStep 2: Ask learner techniques they have seen people use to ensure water is used responsibly\nStep 3: Guide learners to practice using water sparingly\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be taught effects of water wastage with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Caring for water- Using water sparingly at home and school\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to state ways in which water is wasted at home and school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How is water wasted at home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideo clips showing water wastage\nCharts illustrating different practices that lead to water wastage\nPictures on good and bad ways of using water\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.106\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to share experiences on occasions they were facing water scarcity at home and school\nStep 2: Ask learner techniques they have seen people use to ensure water is used responsibly\nStep 3: Guide learners to practice using water sparingly\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite the poem.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be taught effects of water wastage with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Conserving light energy- Sources of light energy at home and in\nschool\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n State different sources of light at home and in school\n Explain responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\n Demonstrate responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can we save light energy in the home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nCharts showing different sources of light\nVideo clips showing different sources of light energy\nPencils, crayons and drawing, light energy conservation poster\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.107-108\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Avail some sources of light such as touches and lamps\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s responsible ways of handling those sources of light.\nStep 3: Ask learners what sources of light they use to see their way around school during the day.\nStep 4: Explain to the learners that it is important to conserve energy to enable others to also benefit from it.\nStep 5: Learners to discuss and share experiences on the various sources of light they use at home and at school at\nnight and during the day.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to walk around the school to examine sources of light energy available in school.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk about ways of conserving light with the help of the parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Conserving light energy- Ways of using light energy in school\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n State different sources of light at home and in school\n Explain responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\n Demonstrate responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can we save light energy in the home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nCharts showing different sources of light\nVideo clips showing different sources of light energy\nPencils, crayons and drawing, light energy conservation poster\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.109\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to download pictures illustrating ways to conserve light\nStep 2: Learners to cut out the pictures and stick them on a Manilla paper\nStep 3: Learners to discuss and share experiences on ways of conserving light energy at school\nStep 4: Guide learners to discuss ways of saving energy shown in pictures on page 50.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nEncourage learners to practice ways of conserving light energy at school.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be encouraged to practice conserving light energy at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Conserving light energy- Ways of using light energy in school\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n State different sources of light at home and in school\n Explain responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\n Demonstrate responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can we save light energy in the home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nCharts showing different sources of light\nVideo clips showing different sources of light energy\nPencils, crayons and drawing, light energy conservation poster\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.109\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to download pictures illustrating ways to conserve light\nStep 2: Learners to cut out the pictures and stick them on a Manilla paper\nStep 3: Learners to discuss and share experiences on ways of conserving light energy at school\nStep 4: Guide learners to discuss ways of saving energy shown in pictures on page 50.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nEncourage learners to practice ways of conserving light energy at school.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be encouraged to practice conserving light energy at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Conserving light energy- Using light energy at home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n State different sources of light at home and in school\n Explain responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\n Demonstrate responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can we save light energy in the home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nCharts showing different sources of light\nVideo clips showing different sources of light energy\nPencils, crayons and drawing, light energy conservation poster\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.110\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to watch video clips of children using light energy appropriately and emulate them\nStep 2: Learners to discuss and share experiences on ways of using light energy responsibly at home.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to draw and colour a sticker on conserving light energy.\nStep 4: Stimulate in class how they can conserve light\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nEncourage learners to practice ways of conserving light energy at school.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be encouraged to practice conserving light energy at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CARE FOR THE ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Conserving light energy- Saving light energy at home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to\n State different sources of light at home and in school\n Explain responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\n Demonstrate responsible ways of using light energy at home and in school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can we save light energy in the home and school?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n Responsibility\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nCharts showing different sources of light\nVideo clips showing different sources of light energy\nPencils, crayons and drawing, light energy conservation poster\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.111\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners into groups\nStep 2: Learners to brainstorm on different rules the parents apply to conserve light.\nStep 3: Guide learners to look at different rules listed down\nStep 4: Ask learners to look for patterns and then consolidate the list\nSUMMARY\nMake summary notes/points\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nEncourage learners to practice ways of conserving light energy at school.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to be encouraged to practice conserving light energy at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "21af20b417a967fe", "path": "lesson-plans/General/LESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES (4).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "General", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 11981, "text": "Environmental all term 1 2 and 3 a href https drive google com uc expo\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- Observing the sky during the day\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen during the day\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the day and night\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 1-3\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Move out with learners to look at objects in the sky during the day. Let learners point out the sun, the\nclouds, the sky and other flying objects.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify that some objects in the sky move while others do not.\nStep 3: Guide learners into observing pictures of the sky, sun and the moon. Learners to identify the objects in\neach picture.\nStep 4: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes pf different objects found in the sky during the day.\nStep 5: Learners to describe what they can see in the sly while outside.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to share their experiences on what they observed in the sky during the day\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe the sky while at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- Observing the sky during the day\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen during the day\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the day and night\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 3-4\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners and recap of the previous lesson\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Move out with learners to look at objects in the sky during the day. Let learners pay attention to the\nlocation and patterns of the objects in the sky\nStep 2: Guide learners into discussion whether the objects are situated in the same place as they were in the\nprevious lesson.\nStep 3: Guide learners into conclusion that objects in the sky come in different shapes, sizes and patterns.\nStep 4: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes of different objects found in the sky during the day.\nStep 5: Learners to describe what they can see in the sly while outside.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to compose and sing a short song about the sky and its objects.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe the sky while at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- Observing the sky during the day\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen during the day\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the day and night\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 3-4\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners and recap of the previous lesson\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Move out with learners to look at objects in the sky during the day. Let learners pay attention to the\nlocation and patterns of the objects in the sky\nStep 2: Guide learners into discussion whether the objects are situated in the same place as they were in the\nprevious lesson.\nStep 3: Guide learners into conclusion that objects in the sky come in different shapes, sizes and patterns.\nStep 4: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes of different objects found in the sky during the day.\nStep 5: Learners to describe what they can see in the sly while outside.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to compose and sing a short song about the sky and its objects.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe the sky while at home\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- Observing the sky at night\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen at night\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the night\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 4--5\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners by asking questions\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into observing the pictures of the night sky.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify that the night-time objects are different from the day-time objects and that’s\nwhat makes the difference between the day and night.\nStep 3: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes of the different objects found in the sky during the\nnight.\nStep 4: Learners to stick the cut objects on a page in their books and label it “night sky”\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to compose and sing a short song about the sky and its objects.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe the sky at night in the company of their parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- Observing the sky at night\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen at night\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the night\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 4--5\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners by asking questions\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into observing the pictures of the night sky.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify that the night-time objects are different from the day-time objects and that’s\nwhat makes the difference between the day and night.\nStep 3: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes of the different objects found in the sky during the\nnight.\nStep 4: Learners to stick the cut objects on a page in their books and label it “night sky”\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to compose and sing a short song about the sky and its objects.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe the sky at night in the company of their parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: ENVIRONMENT AND ITS RESOURCES\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Weather and the Sky- difference I appearance of the sky during\nthe day and night\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe, describe and develop curiosity about all the\nfeatures of the sky as seen at night and during the day\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What do we see when we look at the sky during the day and during the night?\n What differences do we observe in the day and night sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Critical thinking\n Unity\n Respect\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPhotographs, flash cards that show the sky during the night and day\nCrayons, charts and colours for drawing\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg. 6\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners by asking questions\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Greet learners and show them a picture of the sun, moon, stars and clouds and ask them the pictures that\nshow what would be seen in the night sky and day sky.\nStep 2: Guide learners to take a blank piece of paper and divide it in half by drawing a line down the middle with\na pencil\nStep 3: Guide learners to draw, colour and cut out shapes of the different objects found in the sky during the\nnight and day time\nStep 4: Learners to stick the cut objects on the notice board.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to share their experiences on what they learnt concerning the day and the night sky and objects found in\neach of their drawings.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to show their parents the pictures they drew in class.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/