earning)\nStep 1: With the guidance of the teacher learners to visit a nearby home of an elderly and clean the home\nenvironment.\nStep 2: After the outdoor activity learners to share their experiences at the home and find out new ways they\nlearnt that would help them keep their own homes clean.\nStep 3: Learners to share on ways they can keep the environment clean e.g. washing\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to find out things that make the home environment dirty with the help of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Common risks at home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.66-67\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to describe any common injuries that one can suffer at home.\nStep 2: Organize learners in groups and let them listen to stories about people who have been involved in an\naccident\nStep 3: Learners can visit a home where someone suffered an injury.\nStep 4: Learners to share experiences on what they have learnt about common risks at home and safety measures\ntaken in the home.\nStep 5: Guide learners to discuss injuries they have had at home.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to share the stories on risks with their parents/guardians and with friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Common risks at home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.66-67\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to describe any common injuries that one can suffer at home.\nStep 2: Organize learners in groups and let them listen to stories about people who have been involved in an\naccident\nStep 3: Learners can visit a home where someone suffered an injury.\nStep 4: Learners to share experiences on what they have learnt about common risks at home and safety measures\ntaken in the home.\nStep 5: Guide learners to discuss injuries they have had at home.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to share the stories on risks with their parents/guardians and with friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Common risks at home\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.66-67\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to describe any common injuries that one can suffer at home.\nStep 2: Organize learners in groups and let them listen to stories about people who have been involved in an\naccident\nStep 3: Learners can visit a home where someone suffered an injury.\nStep 4: Learners to share experiences on what they have learnt about common risks at home and safety measures\ntaken in the home.\nStep 5: Guide learners to discuss injuries they have had at home.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to share the stories on risks with their parents/guardians and with friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Keeping safe at home.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.68\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to discuss ways they have kept themselves away from harm at home.\nStep 2: Learners to review the common injuries that can happen at home.\nStep 3: Guide learners through various tips that can be used to prevent various injuries.\nStep 4: Learners to share experiences on what they have learnt on safety measures taken in the home.\nStep 5: Guide learners to list ways of keeping safe and secure in the home environment\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to share the stories on risks with their parents/guardians and with friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Keeping safe at home.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.68\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to discuss ways they have kept themselves away from harm at home.\nStep 2: Learners to review the common injuries that can happen at home.\nStep 3: Guide learners through various tips that can be used to prevent various injuries.\nStep 4: Learners to share experiences on what they have learnt on safety measures taken in the home.\nStep 5: Guide learners to list ways of keeping safe and secure in the home environment\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to share the stories on risks with their parents/guardians and with friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Child rights\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.69-70\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in the field and demonstrate different games they play\nStep 2: Guide learners to name meals they eat, and level of parental care at home.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss these rights at home and in school.\nStep 4: Display pictures of different rights and ask learners to identify the child rights.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk to their parents/guardians on child rights.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Child responsibilities\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.70-71\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Review the lesson on child rights. And explain to the learners that for every right there is a responsibility.\nStep 2: Guide learners to watch a video about child responsibilities and discuss how they take up these\nresponsibilities at home.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss different child responsibilities they have learnt.\nStep 4: Guide learners in groups and answer questions on page 32 of their book.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to demonstrate their responsibilities at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Basic needs in the family\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.72\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in pairs to write down a list of things they believe they really need in their lives.\nStep 2: Guide learners to understand what a need is and the basic needs necessary for living.\nStep 3: Guide learners to make posters and charts about different basic needs like a house.\nStep 4: Guide learners to discuss what is happening in each picture on page 33 of their book.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to learn about types of basic needs in the family with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Home environment- Ways parents or guardians meet basic family\nneeds.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Name things found at home\n Participate in caring for things found in the home\n Recognize common risks at home in order to avoid harm to self or others\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n What things are found in the home?\n How can we care for things found in the home?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Self-efficacy\n Learning to learn\n Care\n Respect\nEnvironment education\nSafety and security\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPictures of utensils used at home e.g. cups, spoons and plates, charts showing drawings of tables, chairs, bed, and\nbroom\nArt supplies such as crayons, charts, pencils, Coloured pencils\nSchool kitchen\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.73-74\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Ask learners what they would like to be when they grow up.\nStep 2: Using pictures illustrating different profession, guide learners to understand that people need to work so\nthat they can cater for their basic needs.\nStep 3: Learners to visit a market and observe the goods and services offered and take pictures.\nStep 4: Once back to the classroom distribute the pictures and let learners to write down the things they saw.\nStep 5: Guide learners to identify what the adults are engaging in to meet their needs, in each picture.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to share experiences on what they have learnt on ways of meeting basic needs.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to learn about types of basic needs in the family with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Enterprise projects- Right ways of making money\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Identify and demonstrate awareness of ways of making money rightfully in the family\n Suggest and engage in possible income generating activities at home or school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can the family make money rightfully?\n What activities can generate income for the family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Honesty\n Responsibility\n trustworthiness\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideos showing people working in an office\nCharts showing different types of jobs\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.75-77\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide learners to say what they would like to be when they grow up and how this will help them to make money\nrightfully\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in groups and give them pictures showing people engaged in different jobs\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify the jobs and list down their names.\nStep 3: Learners to discuss experiences of what their parents or guardians do to make money.\nStep 4: Learners to discuss the diverse ways families generate income.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to carry our further research on rightful ways of making money.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk about ways of making money rightfully with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Enterprise projects- Self-employment\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Identify and demonstrate awareness of ways of making money rightfully in the family\n Suggest and engage in possible income generating activities at home or school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can the family make money rightfully?\n What activities can generate income for the family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Honesty\n Responsibility\n trustworthiness\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideos showing people working in an office\nCharts showing different types of jobs\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.77\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to visit livestock farms, crop farms etc.\nStep 2: Guide learners to observe different ways people make money for themselves\nStep 3: Learners to listen to a video clip on stories about how people practice self-employment started their\nbusinesses\nStep 4: Learners to share their observations and opinions.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to carry our further research on rightful ways of making money.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk about ways of making money rightfully with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Enterprise projects- Appropriate income generating activities\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Identify and demonstrate awareness of ways of making money rightfully in the family\n Suggest and engage in possible income generating activities at home or school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can the family make money rightfully?\n What activities can generate income for the family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Honesty\n Responsibility\n trustworthiness\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideos showing people working in an office\nCharts showing different types of jobs\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.78\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to find out more ways of generating income\nStep 2: Guide learners to look at the pictures on page 36 and say how the children are helping their parents to\nmake money in the activities they are involved in.\nStep 3: Learners to find out more from the community members about what they engage in to get money.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to carry our further research on rightful ways of making money.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk about ways of making money rightfully with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Enterprise projects- Appropriate income generating activities\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Identify and demonstrate awareness of ways of making money rightfully in the family\n Suggest and engage in possible income generating activities at home or school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can the family make money rightfully?\n What activities can generate income for the family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Honesty\n Responsibility\n trustworthiness\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideos showing people working in an office\nCharts showing different types of jobs\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.78\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to find out more ways of generating income\nStep 2: Guide learners to look at the pictures on page 36 and say how the children are helping their parents to\nmake money in the activities they are involved in.\nStep 3: Learners to find out more from the community members about what they engage in to get money.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to carry our further research on rightful ways of making money.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to talk about ways of making money rightfully with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nONE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Enterprise projects- other income generating activities\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Identify and demonstrate awareness of ways of making money rightfully in the family\n Suggest and engage in possible income generating activities at home or school\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How can the family make money rightfully?\n What activities can generate income for the family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Honesty\n Responsibility\n trustworthiness\nESD\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nVideos showing people working in an office\nCharts showing different types of jobs\nEnrich Yourself Environmental activities Teachers guide pg.79-80\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/groups\nINTRODUCTION\nDo a brainstorming activity with learners.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners in groups to make model shops behind the class (should be done in 25 lessons)\nStep 2: Guide learners to role play roles of a shopkeeper and a hawker in generating income rightfully\nStep 3: Learners to discuss ways of generating income in families learnt.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary points/notes\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide learners to carry our further research on rightful ways of making money.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to demonstrate ways of establishing income generating activities at home with the help of the\nparents/guardians\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "d9602477cab650de", "path": "lesson-plans/General/LESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES (2).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "General", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 11947, "text": "r Download Basic motor skills a href https drive google com uc export\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – individual skipping\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping.\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate to learners skipping by beginning slowly and then finish fast.\nStep 2: Every learners to have his/her own space to start skipping.\nStep 3: Learners to maintain a good body poise.\nStep 4: Learners to turn with a straight arm, and good head position and shoulders should be maintained\nStep 5: Learners to skip in different ways, skip fast, slow, fast, and slow.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice individual skipping at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – Partner/group skipping\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to :-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping.\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate to learners skipping by beginning slowly and then finish fast.\nStep 2: learners in groups to have their own space to start skipping.\nStep 3: Learners to maintain a good body poise.\nStep 4: Learners to skip on the spot, forward towards the teacher, backwards away from the teacher then skip\nsideways.\nStep 5: Skip as though you are happy, tired, in a hurry.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice partners/grouping with friends at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – circular skipping\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate to learners skipping by beginning slowly and then finish fast.\nStep 2: learners in groups to have their own space to start skipping.\nStep 3: Learners to maintain a good body poise.\nStep 4: Learners to skip in small circles by themselves.\nStep 5: Learners to make some shapes as they skip such as square, triangle.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice circular skipping with friends at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – skipping with partner\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping.\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate to learners skipping by beginning slowly and then finish fast. Do it with a learner.\nStep 2: learners in groups to have their own space to start skipping.\nStep 3: Learners to maintain a good body poise.\nStep 4: Learners to skip with partner and skip around partner.\nStep 5: Learners to skip at the same speed as the partner and learners to do something else while skipping with the\npartner e.g. clapping, holding one hand etc.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice partner skipping with friends at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – skipping in trees\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate to learners skipping by beginning slowly and then finish fast. Do it with a learner.\nStep 2: Two learners to hold a rope behind their bodies and the third to stand between the two.\nStep 3: On signal all three start to skip and jump in unison as they count forward in 5.\nStep 4: Learners to change roles and repeat from step 2.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice skipping in trees with friends at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill (Skipping) – skipping in and out circle\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\n By the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name he parts of the body that are in use when skipping.\n Perform skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Practice skipping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance, balance and for excellence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which part of your body are in use when you are skipping?\n In how many ways can you skip?\n What else can you do with your hands while skipping?\n Which shapes can you make while you are skipping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers, lime or whitewash.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 1-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate the skill first as learners watch\nStep 2: Learners in each group line up behind each other.\nStep 3: On signal the one in front of the line takes a short run and starts to skip in and out of each hoop and the\nruns back to stand behind the last person in line.\nStep 4: The next line to skip only after the team mate has skipped out of the last hope and the group whose\nmembers complete skipping without mistakes is the winner.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to make efforts for further practice.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice skipping in and out circle with friends at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Gallop in a small circle\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform galloping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Establish relationship through galloping for critical thinking and problem solving.\n Appreciate galloping for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which circle can you gallop in?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 12-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into discussion on what they know about galloping.\nStep 2: On free space learners to start to gallop clockwise in small imaginary circle.\nStep 3: Change to galloping anticlockwise and ask them to do something with hands while galloping.\nStep 4: Learners to obey rules as they gallop.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving galloping.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform during prize giving day and AGM.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Gallop in a large circle\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:\n Perform galloping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Establish relationship through galloping for critical thinking and problem solving\n Appreciate galloping for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which circle can you gallop in?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 12-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into discussion on what they know about galloping.\nStep 2: On free space learners to start to gallop clockwise in big imaginary circle.\nStep 3: Change to galloping anticlockwise and ask them to do something with hands while galloping.\nStep 4: Learners to obey rules as they gallop.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving galloping.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform during prize giving day and AGM.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Stealth move\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform galloping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Establish relationship through galloping for critical thinking and problem solving.\n Appreciate galloping for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which circle can you gallop in?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 12-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into discussion on what they know about galloping.\nStep 2: Mark two long lines 20 metres apart.\nStep 3: Learners to take positions behind the line, arm length from each other.\nStep 4: On signal, each learner pretends to be a horse and side gallops to next line.\nStep 5: Repeat the activity and gallop back to start line.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving galloping.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform during prize giving day and AGM.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Gallop over a rope\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform galloping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Establish relationship through galloping for critical thinking and problem solving.\n Appreciate galloping for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which circle can you gallop in?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers, ropes/canes.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 12-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work individually/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners into discussion on what they know about galloping.\nStep 2: Guide two learners in each group to hold the rope.\nStep 3: The other from a distance pf 5 metres, gallop until they come to the rope and gallop over it, the gallop back\nto the team line.\nStep 4: Repeat the steps as the ones who held the ropes to take parts also.\nStep 5: Learners to obey galloping rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving galloping.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform during prize giving day and AGM.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Gallop with a partner\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform galloping in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Establish relationship through galloping for critical thinking and problem solving.\n Appreciate galloping for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Which circle can you gallop in?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers, hopes.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 12-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs/group.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to find a free space within a marked area.\nStep 2: A hoop is placed down, then each learner gallops round the hoop clockwise, turns and gallops\nanticlockwise.\nStep 3: Partners to join hands and gallop together clockwise then anticlockwise and then round each other.\nStep 4: Learners to watch a video on galloping in different directions.\nStep 5: Learners to appreciate galloping as a performance.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving galloping.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform during prize giving day and AGM.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Dodging-\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name the parts of the body that are used when dodging.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Appreciate dodging for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\n Obey rules when playing games for own safety\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Mention games that you can play using dodging?\n In which different ways can you dodge\n What do you do to avoid being hit by a kicked ball?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nVideo.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 19-31.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to watching a video of a netball game.\nStep 2: Guide learners on the dodging styles found in the video.\nStep 3: Learners to name various body parts used in dodging.\nStep 4: Learners to appreciate dodging for safety.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving dodging.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform dodging during various plays.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Dodging- shadow tag and artful dodger\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name the parts of the body that are used when dodging.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Appreciate dodging for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\n Obey rules when playing games for own safety.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Mention games that you can play using dodging?\n In which different ways can you dodge\n What do you do to avoid being hit by a kicked ball?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 19-31.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners in pairs, one of them to run and dodges changing direction frequently and others to pursue him\nand try to step on his/her shadow.\nStep 2: After a while learners to change roles.\nStep 3: One partner to stand behind the other, on “go”, the learners in front runs around changing direction\nregularly.\nStep 4: On signal “stop”, both players freeze.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving dodging.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform dodging during various plays.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Dodging- Dodge and Mark, knee tag\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Name the parts of the body that are used when dodging.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Perform dodging in different ways for strength, coordination, endurance and balance.\n Appreciate dodging for strength, coordination, balance and self-esteem.\n Obey rules when playing games for own safety.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Mention games that you can play using dodging?\n In which different ways can you dodge.\n What do you do to avoid being hit by a kicked ball?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nCitizenship\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nDisaster risk education\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nSports uniform.\nSufficient space.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 19-31.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Partners to face each other.\nStep 2: On signal learners A dodges away, moving sideways or backward. Learners B follows, try to mark\nhim/her, if he/she comes close enough to him/her, give the “halt” signal.\nStep 3: After sometime change over.\nStep 4: Count the number of times you can tag your partner’s knees whilst dodging frequently to avoid being\ntagged yourself\nStep 5: Learners to observe safety while playing and obey rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving dodging.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform dodging during various plays.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: BASIC MOTOR SKILLS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Locomotor skill – Dodging- Tail snatch and zig zag dodge\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learne