 crab stand balance for strength and coordination.\n Practice the crab stand balance for strength and coordination.\n Appreciate performing the crab stand balance for coordination strength and coordination.\n Make relationships when performing the crab stand balance for creativity and imagination.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Name the body parts used in the crab stand balance?\n Name animals that have eight legs?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nMats.\nMarkers.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 161-165.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s Crab stand with support.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform Crab stand with support.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during parents-teachers association meetings and academic.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: GYMNASTICS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dynamic balance: Backward Roll\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform backward roll for strength, balance and coordination.\n Practice backward roll for strength, balance, coordination and excellence.\n Appreciate performing the backward roll for strength, balance and coordination.\n make relationship\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How many times can you roll?\n In which positions can you roll?\n Can you roll from different position?\n In which games do players roll?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFiled markers, bean bags.\nBenches, mats.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 161-165.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s Backward Roll.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform Backward Roll.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during parents-teachers association meetings and academic.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: GYMNASTICS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dynamic balance: Backward Roll down a bench\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n Perform backward roll for strength, balance and coordination.\n Practice backward roll for strength, balance, coordination and excellence.\n Appreciate performing the backward roll for strength, balance and coordination.\n make relationship\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n How many times can you roll?\n In which positions can you roll?\n Can you roll from different position?\n In which games do players roll?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFiled markers, bean bags.\nBenches, mats.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 166-171.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s Backward Roll down a bench.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform Backward Roll down a bench.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during parents-teachers association meetings and academic.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: GYMNASTICS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dynamic balance: crab walk\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n  Name parts of the body that are used in performing the crab walk.\n Perform crab walk for strength and coordination.\n Practice the crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Appreciate perform crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Make relationship while performing.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Do you know the animal called crab?\n How does it move?\n How does one move like a crab?\n Which parts of the body are used when performing the crab walk?\n Which direction is easier to move to during crab walk?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFiled markers, bean bags.\nBalls, digital devices, video clips.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 172-176.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s crab walk.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform crab walk.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during closing days or school cultural festivals.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: GYMNASTICS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dynamic balance: Back support\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n  Name parts of the body that are used in performing the crab walk.\n Perform crab walk for strength and coordination.\n Practice the crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Appreciate perform crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Make relationship while performing.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Do you know the animal called crab?\n How does it move?\n How does one move like a crab?\n Which parts of the body are used when performing the crab walk?\n Which direction is easier to move to during crab walk?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFiled markers, bean bags.\nBalls, digital devices, video clips.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 172-176.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s Back support.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform Back support.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during closing days or school cultural festivals.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MOVEMENT ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: GYMNASTICS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dynamic balance: Three point support\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\n  Name parts of the body that are used in performing the crab walk.\n Perform crab walk for strength and coordination.\n Practice the crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Appreciate perform crab walk for strength, coordination and excellence.\n Make relationship while performing.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n Do you know the animal called crab?\n How does it move?\n How does one move like a crab?\n Which parts of the body are used when performing the crab walk?\n Which direction is easier to move to during crab walk?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Digital literacy\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Peace\n Love\n Unity\n integrity\nLearner support Programme\nHealth education\nLife skills\nCitizenships\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFiled markers, bean bags.\nBalls, digital devices, video clips.\nFoundation Movement activities Teachers guide pg. 172-176.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/ groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to undergo warm-up exercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in specific groups.\nStep 2: Demonstrate to learner’s three point support.\nStep 3: Leaners to perform Three point support.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety rules.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to play digital games involving gymnastics.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice further.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to perform gymnastic displays during closing days or school cultural festivals.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "42f4c2f522d52f21", "path": "lesson-plans/General/Music Lesson Plan.docx", "title": "Music Lesson Plan", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "General", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 1050, "text": "nload Listening and responding a href https drive google com uc export\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: LISTENING AND RESPONDING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Elements of Music (listening and responding)\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Express initial personal reactions to musical performances for self-expression.\nb) Imitate short melodies with accuracy in pitch and rhythm for aural accuracy development.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\na) How does the music make you feel?\nb) How should you feel when you hear this kind of music?\nc) How should you behave when listening to music?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n A repertoire of simple tunes.\n ICT devices such as mobile phones, laptops, tablets.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to listen to live or recorded music.\nStep 2: Learners to tell what they can hear from the music\nStep 3: Conduct some simple vocal exercise. This can be achieved through “solfas” e.g\ndr:m.f:s.f:m.r:dd.m:s.d:s.m:d\nStep 4: Conduct aural dictations to individual and in groups. This can be done as a group competition\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by asking learners to describe their feeling after listening to a piece of music.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing simple melodies accurately in terms of pitch and rhythm.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice at school and describe their feelings about the music they have listened.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: LISTENING AND RESPONDING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Performance Etiquette\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to respond to live or recorded performances with\nappropriate audience behavior to enhance etiquette.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) How do you feel when there is a lot of movement and noise during a musical performance?\n2) What is the importance of good behavior during a musical performance?\n3) As a music performer, how would you feel when there is a lot of interruption?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devises.\n Live/recorded music.\n Performance hall\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work\nINTRODUCTION\nArrange the hall appropriately for the performance.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners on appropriate behavior before and after performance.\nStep 2: Guide learners into watching a video clip of a performance with audience at the background.\nStep 3: Using questions guide learners on the dos and don’ts during performance.\nStep 4: Learners to appreciate music performance.\nSUMMARY:\nPerformance can only be achieved through minimal interruption.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to adjudicate own performances.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to observe appropriate behavior during school function perfomances.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: LISTENING AND RESPONDING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Basic Music Terminology\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to use appropriate music terminology in\nexplaining/discussing music listened to for effective communication.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhy is it important to use proper music terminology?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devises.\n List of music terminologies.\n Recorded/live music performance\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to sing familiar and new songs and use them to discuss the various Music\nterminologies.\nStep 2: Learners to listen to and relate specific music to appropriate events such as national holidays.\nStep 3: Guide learners in discussing various elements of music i.e. loud, soft, fast, slow, high and low.\nDiscuss their effects as well.\nStep 4: Learners to practice singing the songs in small groups.\nSUMMARY:\nReview the lesson and note summary points.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer questions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to prepare one or two songs to be presented in a community cultural festival or in a school\nfunction.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nOne\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: LISTENING AND RESPONDING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Music Appreciation\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Appreciate and enjoy music drawn from diverse cultures and time through digital devices.\nb) Relate specific music to appropriate events in the community for effective communication.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. What does the music that you hear make you think about?\n2. How does the music that you hear make you feel?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n Learning to learn\n respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devises.\n Internet.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nIdentify the music from varied cultures to be played for appreciation.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners to listen to the music identified.\nStep 2: Learners to respond and react to the music freely with it before discussion.\nStep 3: Guide learners into a discussion about the music listened to, relating to appropriate events such\nas joyful celebrations.\nStep 4: Learners to appreciate the music they have listened to.\nSUMMARY:\nReview the lesson and note the summary points.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer questions related to the lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nAssign individual learners a project to be done out of school where they listen to music played on\ntelevision, radio or over the internet with the help of the parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "bbb09c3180ced77a", "path": "lesson-plans/General/Psychomotor Lesson Plan.docx", "title": "Psychomotor Lesson Plan", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "General", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 11275, "text": "Psychomotor activities all term 3 a href https drive google com uc exp\nTERM 3\nWEEK 1: LESSON 1\nSCHOOL\nCLASS\nDATE\nTIME\nROLL\nStrand /Theme\n: Swimming.\nSub strand /sub-Theme:\nWater orientation.\nSpecific learning outcome:\nBy the end of the end of the sub strand the learner\nshould be able to:\nPerform the front float.\nKey inquiry question\nDo you know how to kick in water?\nCore Learning resources:\nChildren,Pool,Know more teachers’\nguide pg287.\nOrganization of Learning\n:  Outdoor Activitiesindividual\nand group work.\nTIME\nCONTENT\n5 minutes\n25minutes\nIntroduction\nWarm up activity.\nLearners to perform a warm up activity.\nStretch themselves.\nLesson development\nStep 1\nDemonstrate to learners how to float using front part.\n5 minutes\nCore\ncompetence:\nCommunication\nand\ncollaboration-Learners\nwill\nplay\nall\nthe\ngames\nin\npairs.Imagination\nand\ncreativity-Learners\nwill\ndevelop\nimagination\nand\ncreativity as they play.\nValues:Respect, cooperation and social justice as\nthey practice how to float together.\nLink\nto\nPcls:\nLifeskills-coping\nwith\nemotions\nas\nthey\nexpress\ntheir\nfeelings\nwhile\nswimming.\nStep 2\nAsk learners to form a line. Assist them to float\nin water using front part of the body.\nCore\ncompetence:\nCommunication\nand\ncollaboration-Learners\nwill\nplay\nall\nthe\ngames\nin\npairs.Imagination\nand\ncreativity-Learners\nwill\ndevelop\nimagination\nand\ncreativity as they play.\nValues:Respect, cooperation and social justice as\nthey practice how to float together.\nLink\nto\nPcls:\nLifeskills-coping\nwith\nemotions\nas\nthey\nexpress\ntheir\nfeelings\nwhile\nswimming.\nStep 3\nLearners to  float on water using front part.\nCore\ncompetence:\nCommunication\nand\ncollaboration-Learners\nwill\nplay\nall\nthe\ngames\nin\npairs.Imagination\nand\ncreativity-Learners\nwill\ndevelop\nimagination\nand\ncreativity as they play.\nValues:Respect, cooperation and social justice as\nthey practice how to float together.\nLink\nto\nPcls:\nLifeskills-coping\nwith\nemotions\nas\nthey\nexpress\ntheir\nfeelings\nwhile\nswimming.\nConclusion\nRelaxation.\nAsk learners to slowly kick while in the water.\nAsk the questions e.g How did you feel in water?\nExtended Activities\nLearners to practice how to float on water at home.\nSummary:\nLearners to identify how to float.\nReflection in the lesson/self-remarks: ..........................................................................................\nWEEK 1: LESSON 2\nSCHOOL\nCLASS\nDATE\nTIME\nROLL\nStrand /Theme\n: Swimming.\nSub strand /sub-Theme:\nWater orientation.\nSpecific learning outcome:\nBy the end of the end of the sub strand the learner\nshould be able to:\nFloat on his/her back\nKey inquiry question\nWhat did you like about the activity?\nCore Learning resources:\nChildren,Pool,Know more teachers’\nguide pg290.\nOrganization of Learning\n:  Outdoor Activitiesindividual\nand group work.\nTIME\nCONTENT\n5 minutes\nIntroduction\n25minutes\n5 minutes\nWarm up activity.\nLearners to perform a warm up activity.\nStretch themselves. Guide learners to sprinkle water\non themselves.\nLesson development\nStep 1\nDemonstrate to learners how to float on their back.\nCore\ncompetence:\nCommunication\nand\ncollaboration-Learners\nwill\nplay\nall\nthe\ngames\nin\npairs.Imagination\nand\ncreativity-Learners\nwill\ndevelop\nimagination\nand\ncreativity\nas they play.\nValues:Respect, cooperation and social justice as\nthey float on water.\nLink to Pcls: Lifeskills-coping with emotions as the\nfloat on water.\nStep 2\nAsk learners to form a line. Assist them to float\nin water on their back.\nCore\ncompetence:\nCommunication\nand\ncollaboration-Learners\nwill\nplay\nall\nthe\ngames\nin\npairs.Imagination\nand\ncreativity-Lea