o appreciate their physical appearance as uniquely created by God.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you like most about yourself?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n cooperation\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 1-5.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg. 12-14.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song on how they are God’s special creation.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Help learners to build self-esteem by helping them appreciate themselves as God’s creation.\nStep 2: Educate learners on the importance of using body parts to serve the Lord.\nStep 3: Learners to read Psalms 139:13\nStep 4: Learners in pairs to observe and state in front of the class their similarities and differences.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Psalms 139:13\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to visit a senior citizen to talk about different gender roles in the community.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Self-awareness\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to state different chores they do at home as service to God..\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you like doing when you are free?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n cooperation\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 6-11\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg. 12-13\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to role play different chores they do at home.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Help learners to build self-esteem by helping them appreciate themselves as God’s creation.\nStep 2: Educate learners on the importance of using body parts to serve the Lord.\nStep 3: Learners to read Psalms 139:13\nStep 4: Guide learners on the use of their bodies in serving God and helping at home.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Psalms 139:13 and Genesis 1:27\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to visit a senior citizen to talk about different gender roles in the community.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Self-awareness\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to state different chores they do at home as service to God.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you like doing when you are free?\nAppreciate their worth as part of God’s creation?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n cooperation\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 12-14.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg. 12-13.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to role play different chores they do at home.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Help learners to build self-esteem by helping them appreciate themselves as God’s creation.\nStep 2: Help learners to understand why they should worship and serve God as part of His creation.\nStep 3: Learners to read Psalms 139:13 and know how our abilities vary in near-infinite ranges.\nStep 4: Guide learners to understand what makes them special creation before God\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Psalms 139:13 and Genesis 1:27\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to visit a senior citizen to talk about different gender roles in the community.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: My Family\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify members of the extended family to enhance a sense of\nbelonging.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWho are the members in your extended family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 15-20.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg15-16.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Acts 10:2\nStep 2: Guide learners to name the family members in their extended family.\nStep 3: Learners to draw and colour members of their extended family.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Acts 10:2\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in family gathering and enquire from their parents, name of members of their extended\nfamily.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: My Family\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to appreciate members of their extended family for harmonious\nliving.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWho are the members in your extended family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 15-20.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg15-16.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Acts 10:2.\nStep 2: Guide learners to sort photographs of members of the extended family.\nStep 3: Guide learners to in discussing how they relate with members of the extended family i.e. through\nsharing.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Acts 10:2.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in family gathering and enquire from their parents, name of members of their extended\nfamily.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: My Family\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to state items shared at home for family unity.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWho are the members in your extended family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 15-20.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg15-16.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Acts 10:2.\nStep 2: Guide learners to sort photographs of members of the extended family.\nStep 3: Guide learners to in discussing how they relate with members of the extended family i.e. through\nsharing.\nStep 4: Learners to mention items they share at home for harmonious living.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Acts 10:2.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in family gathering and enquire from their parents, name of members of their extended\nfamily.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: My Family\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to state items not to be shared at home for healthy living.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWho are the members in your extended family?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 15-20.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg15-16.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Acts 10:2.\nStep 2: Guide learners to sort photographs of members of the extended family.\nStep 3: Guide learners to in discussing how they relate with members of the extended family i.e. through\nsharing.\nStep 4: Learners to mention items that they should not share at home.\nStep 5: Help learners to understand the importance of sharing items at home and state why certain items should\nnot be shared.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary of the sub strand.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Acts 10:2.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in family gathering and enquire from their parents, name of members of their extended\nfamily.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: CREATION OF THE SKY, SUN, MOON AND THE STARS.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to recognize God as the creator of the sky, sun, moon and stars and\nrevere him.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you see when you observe the sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nGlue.\nScissors.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 21-27.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg17-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song on creation.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Genesis 1:14-19.\nStep 2: Learners to observe the sky.\nStep 3: Guide learners in discussing the importance of creation.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Genesis 1:16\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in observing the sky at night with the help of their parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: CREATION OF THE SKY, SUN, MOON AND THE STARS.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to recognize God as the creator of the sky, sun, moon and stars and\nrevere him.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you see when you observe the sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nGlue.\nScissors.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 21-27.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg17-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song on creation.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Genesis 1:14-19.\nStep 2: Learners to observe the sky.\nStep 3: Guide learners in discussing the importance of creation.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson and make summary\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Genesis 1:16\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in observing the sky at night with the help of their parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: CREATION OF THE SKY, SUN, MOON AND THE STARS.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to draw and colour the sun, moon and stars as to appreciate God’s\ncreation.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do you see when you observe the sky?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Problem solving\n self-awareness\n Friendship formation\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nCritical thinking\ncitizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBible.\nGlue.\nScissors.\nC.R.E activities Learner’s Book Grade 2 pg. 21-27.\nC.R.E Activities Teacher’s Guide Grade 2 pg17-18.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song about the sky, moon and stars as God’s creation.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to read Genesis 1:14-19 and 1:16.\nStep 2: Learners to observe the sky.\nStep 3: Guide learners to draw and colour the moon, the sun and stars. They should cut and mount pictures of\nthe sun, moon and stars on a chart.\nStep 4: Learners to appreciate creation as part of Gods work.\nSUMMARY\nGod is the creator.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to recite Genesis 1:16\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to participate in observing the sky at night with the help of their parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "13d6752605808685", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES.docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 3272, "text": "noopener Download The Church a href https drive google com uc export d\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: the Lord’s Prayer\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to recite the Lord’s Prayer and apply it in their daily lives.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhich prayer did Jesus teach his disciples?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Love\n Faith\n Trust\nCitizenship\nPatriotism\nCritical thinking\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nCharts containing the Lord’s Prayer\nFlash cards.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 90-92.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Direct learners to the image on page 90 of the learner’s book.\nStep 2: Select some learners and help them to find and read Mathew 6:9-13.\nStep 3: Ask learners to say the first four lines of the Lord’s Prayer that they had learnt in Grade 2.\nStep 4: Using questions take learners through the prayer, helping them understand what it contains.\nStep 5: Ask the learners if they will say the Lord’s Prayer from now on. Ask learners to keep their books and\ndistribute papers with sentences of the prayer to each group.\nStep 6: Learners to work as a group to arrange the papers in the correct order.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners to lead others in saying the Lord’s Prayer during assembly and other occasions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: the Lord’s Prayer\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to recite the Lord’s Prayer and apply it in their daily lives.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhich prayer did Jesus teach his disciples?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Love\n Faith\n Trust\nCitizenship\nPatriotism\nCritical thinking\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nCharts containing the Lord’s Prayer.\nFlash cards.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 90-92.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to recall that Matthew 6:9-13.\nStep 2: Use questions to recognize the responsibility we have of forgiving others in the same way that God forgives\nus.\nStep 3: Lead learners to commit to saying the Lord’s Prayer during various occasions and times in their lives.\nStep 4: Direct learners to activity 1. Put the learners in groups and distribute the flash cards to the groups.\nStep 5: Guide learners through the task.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners to lead others in saying the Lord’s Prayer during assembly and other occasions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: the Lord’s Prayer\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to recite the Lord’s Prayer and apply it in their daily lives.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhich prayer did Jesus teach his disciples?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Citizenship\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Love\n Faith\n Trust\nCitizenship\nPatriotism\nCritical thinking\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nCharts containing the Lord’s Prayer.\nFlash cards.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 90-92.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to remember from the verse that saying the Lord’s Prayer is how Jesus wants us to pray.\nStep 2: Use questions to guide the learners to know that the Lord’s Prayer has everything we need to say to God.\nStep 3: Direct learners to activity 2. Put learners in groups and ask them to do the activity that is reciting prayer in\nKiswahili.\nStep 4: Learners to recite the prayer as a class.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners to lead others in saying the Lord’s Prayer during assembly and other occasions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Showing respect to God during Prayer\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to show respect to God during prayer by adopting different postures.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you show respect to God when praying?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Love\n Humility\nCitizenship\nPatriotism\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nPictures of different postures praying, some are showing respect while other are not.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 90-92.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Ask learners to sing the national anthem. Observe their posture.\nStep 2: Using step one, bring the fact that as they showed respect as they say the anthem, they must do so when\npraying.\nStep 3: Direct learners to activity 3 on page 92 of the learner’s book.\nStep 4: Use questions to remind the learners that the Lord’s prayer requires respect us to have humility as we honour\nGod and accept that his will be done.\nStep 5: Guide the learners to understand why it is important to show respect to God when praying.\nStep 6: Lead learners to say ways in which they will show respect to God as they pray. Using pictures collected,\nshow learners and ask them if the people are showing respect to God or not.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners to lead others in saying the Lord’s Prayer during assembly and other occasions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Showing respect to God during Prayer\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to show respect to God during prayer by adopting different postures.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you show respect to God when praying?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Love\n Humility\nCitizenship\nPatriotism\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible\nPictures of different postures praying, some are showing respect while other are not.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 90-92\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Ask learners what happens when they ask for something in a rude way and when they ask in a humble and\nrespectful way.\nStep 2: Ask learners if they have prayed since the last session.\nStep 3: Using questions remind learners of the need to be humble and respectful during prayer as indicated by the\nfirst four lines of the Lord’s Prayer.\nStep 4: Lead learners into appreciating that just as they are likely to get their requests from others when they are\nhumble and respectful, they should pray to God in a humble and respectful way.\nStep 5: Ask learners how they are going to pray to God going forward.\nStep 6: Direct learners to activity 4. Put the learners in groups and ask the to do- demonstrate how they will show\nrespect to God when saying the Lord’s Prayer.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question, direct on take home activity and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners to lead others in saying the Lord’s Prayer during assembly and other occasions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The coming of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to discuss the coming of the Holy Spirit on the day of Pentecost and\nrelate it to their lives.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat happened on the day of Pentecost?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Learning to learn\n Unity\n Sharing\n Integrity\n courage\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nCandle.\nMatchbox.\nLaptop, projector.\nVideo of the day of Pentecost.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 94-95.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: At a safe space on your desk, place the candle and light it. Let the learners say if they have ever seen such a\nflame hovering on someone’s head.\nStep 2: Bring the key inquiry questions to the attention of the learners.\nStep 3: Direct learners to activity 1. Have them look at the image before attempting to answer questions that follow.\nStep 4: Select learners and help them to find and read out loud Acts 2:1-4.\nStep 5: Guide learners to recall that in grade 2 they learnt about the promise Jesus gave to his disciples.\nStep 6: Use questions to get details of what happened on Pentecost. Lead learners to discuss what they think and feel\nabout what happened on the day of Pentecost.\nStep 7: Use leading questions to get learners to appreciate the gift of the Holy Spirit and direct learners to activity 2\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question, direct on take home activity and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The coming of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to discuss the coming of the Holy Spirit on the day of Pentecost and\nrelate it to their lives.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat happened on the day of Pentecost?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Learning to learn\n Unity\n Sharing\n Integrity\n courage\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nCandle.\nMatchbox.\nLaptop, projector.\nVideo of the day of Pentecost.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 94-95\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Select some learners to read the Acts 2:1-4 and others listen.\nStep 2: Guide learners in watching the video on the day of Pentecost.\nStep 3: Use questions and leading statements to emphasize that the coming of the Holy Spirit was a fulfilment of the\npromise Jesus had made earlier to his disciples and other believers.\nStep 4: Guide the learners to commit to keeping their promises like Jesus did.\nStep 5: After watching the video, ask learners to talk about what they saw in the video.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question, direct on take home activity and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The fruit of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to appreciate the fruit of the Holy Spirit by living harmoniously with\nothers.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhich are some of the fruit of the Holy Spirit?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Love\n Sharing\n Unity\n Co-operation\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nSelf-esteem\nInterpersonal relationship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nFlash cards with the fruit of the Holy Spirit and others with vices, crayons.\nOld newspapers, Colored and plain papers.\nCellotape, strings.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 95-96.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Bring the key inquiry questions to the attention of the learners.\nStep 2: Direct the learners to activity 1. Give them time to conduct the activities 1 and 2.\nStep 3: Select some learners to read Galatians 5:22 out loud as the others listen.\nStep 4: Use questions and leading statements to help the learners understand the concept of the fruit of the Holy\nSpirit. Use the analogy of the importance of normal fruits to our bodies to help with the explanation.\nStep 5: Guide learners to desire to have the fruit of the Holy Spirit because of the good they bring. Direct learners to\nactivity 5. Put them in groups to carry out the task.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question, direct on take home activity and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The fruit of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to appreciate the fruit of the Holy Spirit by living harmoniously with\nothers.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhich are some of the fruit of the Holy Spirit?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Love\n Sharing\n Unity\n Co-operation\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nSelf-esteem\nInterpersonal relationship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nFlash cards with the fruit of the Holy Spirit and others with vices, crayons.\nOld newspapers, Colored and plain papers.\nCellotape, strings.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 95-96.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Using questions, remind learners of what they read from the Bible and from their textbooks in the previous\nlesson.\nStep 2: Revisit the explanation of the concept of the fruit of the Holy Spirit as you did in previous lesson..\nStep 3: Guide learners to see the importance of having the fruit of the Holy Spirit. Guide them to express desire to\nhave the fruit of the Holy Spirit in their lives.\nStep 4: Ask learners to sit in groups, distribute the modelling materials to each group and ask learners to model the\nfruits of the Holy Spirit.\nStep 5: Ask learners to display their work.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to ask and answer question, direct on take home activity and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The work of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to appreciate the fruit of the Holy Spirit by living harmoniously with\nothers.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat is the work of the Holy Spirit in the life of a Christian?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Love\n Sharing\n Unity\n Co-operation\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nSelf-esteem\nInterpersonal relationship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nFlash cards with the fruit of the Holy Spirit.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 97-99.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Direct learners to activity 6. Give them time to look at the pictures before attempting to answer the questions\nthat follow.\nStep 2: Bring the key inquiry question to the attention of the learners.\nStep 3: Using questions, remind the learners of the fruit of the Holy Spirit\nStep 4: Ask a learners to name a fruit of the Holy Spirit and ask others to explain what that fruit would enable them\nto do in life.\nStep 5: Guide the learners to say how they will use the fruit of the Holy Spirit to do good in their lives.\nStep 6: Pair up learners and direct them to carry out activity 7\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nDirect learners to the I wonder section and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CRE ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: THE CHURCH\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: The work of the Holy Spirit\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to appreciate the fruit of the Holy Spirit by living harmoniously with\nothers.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat is the work of the Holy Spirit in the life of a Christian?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Love\n Sharing\n Unity\n Co-operation\nCitizenship\nSocial cohesion\nSelf-esteem\nInterpersonal relationship\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nGood news bible.\nFlash cards with the fruit of the Holy Spirit.\nLearner’s Book 3 page 97-99.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION.\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Ask the learners to mention things they have done that they believe is the work of the Holy Spirit in their\nlives.\nStep 2: Lead learners in recalling the fruit of the Holy Spirit as listed in the Bible verse.\nStep 3: Let learners talk about what they will do to show the work of the Holy Spirit in them.\nStep 4: Direct learners to activity 8 and guide them through the activity.\nStep 5: Ask learners to answer the Exercise 5 questions in their books, individually\nSUMMARY\nLearners to read summary notes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nDirect learners to the I wonder section and inform them of the next lesson.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nEncourage learners and help them make it a habit or even form a club that does work that reflect a particular fruit of the\nHoly Spirit according to their abilities..\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "a8d314b14d0091b0", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES (2).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 2081, "text": "noopener Download Sculpture a href https drive google com uc export do\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Toys: Making simple Toys of Houses.\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n1. Observe samples of toys, materials and tools from teacher’s collection and or digital media to motivate them make\ntheir own.\n2. Collect different types of tools from the environment that can be used to make toys.\n3. Make simple toys of houses using locally available materials and tools to play with for fun.\n4. Display and discuss their toys for self-efficiency.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What toys can you see in the class inquiry corner?\n Which materials and tools are used for constructing and assembling simple toys of house?\n How do you do with toys?\n What do you like about toys of houses?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample toys, locally available materials and tools, stick, strings, threat, rubber bands, grass, pieces\nof waste clothe, cotton wool, old newspapers,\nWaste paper, wire, straws, banana fiber bottle tops, pair of scissor, ICT devices and any other\ndevices.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 70-76.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample toys, material and tools from your collection.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different types of toys.\nStep 3: Provide learners with materials and tools and Guide learners them to make simple toys.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety precautions when using the items.\nSUMMARY\nSculpture is the process or technique of creating three dimensional forms for modelling, carving, molding or\nconstruction.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community with the help of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Toys: Making simple Toy cars\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n1. Observe sample of car toys, material and tools from teacher’s collection and or digital media to motivate them make\ntheirs.\n2. Collect different types of tools and materials from the environment that can be used to make toy car.\n3. Make sample toy car using locally available materials and tools to play with for fun.\n4. Display and talk about own and others toys in class for self-efficacy.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What toys can you see in the class inquiry corner?\n What materials and tools are used for constructing and assembling simple toy cars?\n What do you do with toy car?\n What do you like about toy car?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample toys, locally available materials and tools, stick, strings, threat, rubber bands, grass, pieces\nof waste clothe, cotton wool, old newspapers,\nWaste paper, wire, straws, banana fiber bottle tops, pair of scissor, ICT devices and any other\ndevices.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 70-76.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample toys, material and tools from your collection.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different types of toys.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners in making their own toy cars.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety precautions when using the items.\nSUMMARY\nSculpture is the process or technique of creating three dimensional forms for modelling, carving, molding or\nconstruction.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community with the help of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Toys: Making Animal Toys\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n1. Observe sample of animal toys from the teacher’s collection and or digital media to motivate them make their own.\n2. Identify simple animal toys, locally available materials and tools for making toys at inquiry corner for motivating.\n3. Make simple animal toys using locally available materials and tools to play with for fun.\n4. Display their toys for class discussion to appreciate own and other works.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What toys can you see in the class inquiry corner?\n Which materials and tools do we use in making simple animals toys?\n What can you do with animal toy\n What do you like about animal toys?\n Which animal toys will you like to play with?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample toys, locally available materials and tools, stick, strings, threat, rubber bands, grass, pieces\nof waste clothe, cotton wool, old newspapers,\nWaste paper, wire, straws, banana fiber bottle tops, pair of scissor, ICT devices and any other\ndevices.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 70-76\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of animal toys, material and tools from your collection.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different types of toys.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners in making their own toy of animals\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety precautions when using the items.\nSUMMARY\nSculpture is the process or technique of creating three dimensional forms for modelling, carving, molding or\nconstruction.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community with the help of parents\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Kites: Making simple kites using local materials\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Observe sample of kites, locally available materials and tools for making kites at the teacher’s collection for\nmotivation.\n Observe kites materials and tools for making kites from the digital media to motivate them make their own.\n Individually explore materials to construct and assemble simple kites.\n Display their simple kites and talk about them in class for appreciation.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What materials and tools are used for constructing and assembling kites?\n What safety precautions should be taken as they make the kites?\n How do you play with a kites?\n What time do you fly kites?\n What do you like with the kites you made?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample kites, old newspaper, magazines, adhesive (pastes glue) ,\nCrayons, pieces of curling ribbons, strings, threads, found materials, rubber bands, ICT devices and\nany other relevant resources.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 77-82\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of kites, locally available materials and tools for making kites.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different materials used for making kites.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners in making their own kites.\nStep 4: Learners to fly their assembled kites.\nStep 5: Learners to observe safety precautions.\nSUMMARY\nKites are toys of a light frame with thin material stretched over it.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community for use in making kites.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Kites: Making kites using Coloured paper.\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Observe sample of kites, materials and tools from teacher’s collection and or digital media to motivate them make\ntheir own.\n Collect different types of materials and tools from the environment that can be used to make kites.\n Make simple kites using Coloured paper to play with for fun.\n Display their kites as a group discussion.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What materials and tools are used for constructing and assembling kites?\n What safety precautions should be taken as they make kites?\n How do you play with a kites?\n What time do we fly kites?\n What do you like with the kites you made?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample kites, old newspaper, magazines, adhesive (pastes glue),\nCrayons, pieces of curling ribbons, strings, threads, found materials, rubber bands, ICT devices and\nany other relevant resources.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 77-82.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of kites, locally available materials and tools for making kites.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different materials used for making kites.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners in making their own simple kites using Coloured paper.\nStep 4: Learners to fly their assembled kites Learners to observer safety precautions.\nSUMMARY\nKites are toys of a light frame with thin material stretched over it.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community for use in making kites.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: SCULPTURE\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Kites: Making simple kites from cloth material\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Observe sample of kites, materials and tools from teacher’s collection and or digital media to motivate them make\ntheir own.\n Collect different types of materials and tools from them the environment that can be used to make kites.\n Make simple kites using cloth material to play with for fun.\n Display their kites as a group for class discussion.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n What materials and tools are tools are used for constructing and assembling kites?\n What safety precautions should be taken as they make kites?\n How do you assemble and construct a kite using cloth materials?\n Which cloth materials are appropriate for constructing kites?\n What’d you like with the kites made of cloth?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample kites, old newspaper, magazines, adhesive (pastes glue).\nCrayons, pieces of curling ribbons, strings, threads, found materials, rubber bands, ICT devices and\nany other relevant resources.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 77-82.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of kites made from clothes, locally available materials and tools for making kites.\nStep 2: Guide learners to name and discuss different materials used for making kites.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners in making their own simple kites using cloth material.\nStep 4: Learners to fly their assembled kites Learners to observer safety precautions.\nSUMMARY\nKites are toys of a light frame with thin material stretched over it.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to collect plastic, bottle tops and old newspapers from the community for use in making kites.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "b4bea30a292b6bb7", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES (3).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 1645, "text": "noopener Download Paper craft a href https drive google com uc export\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Curling technique: make curled paper items freely\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify sample paper item made using the curling method for fun and motivation.\n Experiment with tools and materials used in making curled paper decoration for beauty and self –expression.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Have ever seen items using curled paper?\n How do you make curled paper items?\n Would you want to make decorations using curled papers?\n How would you want your curled paper items to be used?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nPapers, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesive ,\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 96-99\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of curled paper items.\nStep 2: Provide and assortment of papers to learners and Guide learners on curling method of paper craft.\nStep 3: Guide learners into carrying out the curling.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures.\nSUMMARY\nThe art of curling paper involves raisin flat paper into various shapes.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community project to collect re-usable waste paper from their community for use in paper\nactivities.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Curling technique: make two Coloured items using the curling method\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Use appropriate materials and tools for making cut paper decoration.\n Make colored items using the cutting method for self-fulfillment and enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Have you ever seen items made using the cutting method of paper craft?\n Would you want to make decorations using cut papers?\n What colours would you want to use on your decoration?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nPapers, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesive,\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 96-99\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of Coloured curled paper items.\nStep 2: Provide and assortment of papers to learners and Guide learners on curling method of paper craft.\nStep 3: Demonstrate the use of colour and Guide learners to make Coloured paper items.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures.\nSUMMARY\nThe art of curling paper involves raisin flat paper into various shapes.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community project to collect re-usable waste paper from their community for use in paper\nactivities.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Cutting technique\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Experiment on tools and materials used in making cut out paper decorations.\n Make free decorations using the cutting method.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Have you ever seen items made using the cutting method of paper craft?\n Would you want to make decorations using cut papers?\n What colours would you want to use on your decorations?\n How would you want paper decorations to be used?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nAssortment of paper, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesives,\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 100-104.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of Coloured curled paper items.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify materials and tools used in cutting method.\nStep 3: Demonstrate the use of cutting to make decorations and let learners make samples.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures.\nSUMMARY\nThe art of curling paper involves raising flat paper into various shapes.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community project to collect re-usable waste paper from their community for use in paper\nactivities.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Making colour paper items using cutting method.\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Use appropriate materials and tools for making cut paper decorations.\n Make Coloured items using the cutting method for self fulfilment and enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Have you ever seen items made using the cutting method of paper craft?\n Would you want to make decorations using cut papers?\n What colours would you want to use on your decorations?\n How would you want paper decorations to be used?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nAssortment of paper, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesives,\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 100-104.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of Coloured curled paper items.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify materials and tools used in cutting method.\nStep 3: Demonstrate the use of cutting to make decorations by using colors.\nStep 4: Learners to experiment the use of colour and answer questions.\nSUMMARY\nThe art of curling paper involves raisin flat paper into various shapes.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community project to collect re-usable waste paper from their community for use in paper\nactivities.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Making paper pleated decorations freely\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify sample paper items made using the pleating method for fun and motivation.\n Experiment with tools and materials used in making paper pleated decoration for beauty and self-fulfillment.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Where do we find pleated paper decorations in our environment?\n How do pleated paper decoration look like?\n How do you make pleated paper decorations?\n Would you like to make pleated paper decorations?\n Which colors would you want to use on your pleated paper item?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nAssortment of paper, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesives.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 105-109.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of pleated items from the physical and digital environment.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify materials and tools used in pleating method.\nStep 3: Demonstrate the use the pleating method and allow learners to experiment with materials and tools.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to ask and answer questions.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community project to collect re-usable waste paper from their community for use in paper\nactivities\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: PAPER CRAFT\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Making Coloured decoration using the paper pleating method.\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Review materials, tools and pleated papers items samples for motivation, to make their own.\n Make colored items using the pleating method for decoration and self-fulfillment.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Where do we find pleated paper decorations in our environment?\n How do paper pleated decorations look like?\n How do you make pleated decoration paper?\n Would you like to make pleated paper decorations?\n What colors would want to use on your pleated papers items?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Creativity and imagination\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nAssortment of paper, pair of scissors, cutting blades,\nPencils, rulers, adhesives,\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 105-109.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of Coloured pleated items from the physical and digital environment.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify materials and tools used in pleating method.\nStep 3: Demonstrate the use the pleating method and allow learners to experiment using colour.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to ask and answer questions.\nCONCLUSION\nTake learners through demonstration of how to make a peacock.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to undertake a community based project to take care of the environment.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "4023ecde7e629715", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES.docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 1762, "text": "sson Plans Weaving a href https drive google com uc export download\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Serrated Card Loom weaving\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify woven items in the physical and or digital media for motivation.\n Make a serrated card loom for weaving a table mat.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n How do we identify materials used in serrated card loom weaving?\n How do we prepare a serrated card loom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nSample serrated card loom, sample woven items, and stiff papers such as carton box,\nmanila paper ,ruler ,woven articles such as table mat ,floor mat /rag or cloths ,cutting tools such\nas safety scissors ICT devices.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 83-89.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of serrated card loom.\nStep 2: Guide learners to observe and identify serrated card loom, materials used in weaving.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to make a serrated card loom.\nStep 4: Learners to store safely the card loom made for the next lesson.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven items such as coasters through the guidance of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Serrated Card Loom weaving\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify woven items and weaving materials in the physical and or digital media for motivation.\n Fix wrap on a serrated card loom for weaving.\n Weave a two color table mat for self-expression.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n How do we fix thread on a serrated card loom?\n How do you weave the second line when making table mat?\n How do we weave using a serrated card loom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nWoven articles, ICT devices, serrated card looms, cutting tools,\nRaffia, banana fibers, reeds, grass strips of fabric, or t shirt materials, thread, stings.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 83-89.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of serrated card loom.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify woven articles and weaving materials.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to fix the warp on a serrated card loom and how to weave.\nStep 4: Guide learners to fix the warp thread and weave at least 5 lines.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven items such as coasters through the guidance of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Serrated Card Loom weaving\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify woven items from the teacher’s collection and or digital media for motivation.\n Weave two color table mat using a serrated card loom for aesthetic and self-expression.\n Display and appreciate own and others work.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n How do you weave a table mat in two colors?\n How do you make sure the table mat is firm and tight?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nWoven articles, ICT devices, serrated card looms, cutting tools,\nRaffia, banana fibers, reeds, grass strips of fabric, or t shirt materials, thread, stings.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 83-89\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of woven materials from the teacher’s collection.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify woven articles and weaving materials.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to weave and do finishing on a table mat.\nStep 4: Learners to observe safety measures when using the materials.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven items such as coasters through the guidance of parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Dowel Loom Weaving: making a dowel loom\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify woven items in the physical and or digital media for motivation.\n Identify tools and materials for weaving using a dowel.\n Make a dowel loom for weaving a table mat.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n Which materials from the local environment that can be to make a dowel?\n How do we make a dowel loom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nWoven articles, dowel loom, sticks, banana fiber, reeds, rope,\nStrings, strips of fabric, cutting tools, ICT devices, sticks.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 90-95\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of woven items from teacher’s collection and or digital media.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify and name materials for making a dowel loom and for weaving.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to make a dowel loom.\nStep 4: Learners to individually make dowel looms and store them for the next lesson.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven table mats that can be used at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Dowel Loom Weaving: warping the loom\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n1. Identify dowel loom and woven items in the physical and or digital media for motivation.\n2. Fix warp thread on a dowel loom for weaving.\n3. Weave a two color table mat for self-expression.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n How do we fix warp thread on a dowel loom?\n How do we weave using a dowel loom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nWoven articles, dowel loom, sticks, banana fiber, reeds, rope,\nStrings, strips of fabric, cutting tools, ICT devices, sticks.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 90-95\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of woven items from teacher’s collection and or digital media.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify and name materials for making a dowel loom and for weaving.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to fix warp thread on a dowel loom.\nStep 4: Learners to weave at least 5 lines on the dowel loom and store them for the next lesson.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven table mats that can be used at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN CREATIVE ARTS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND: WEAVING\nSUB STRAND: FORMS: Dowel Loom Weaving\nSPECIFIC LEARNING OUTCOMES\nBy the end of the lessons, the learner should be able to:-\n Identify woven items from teacher’s collection and or digital media for motivation.\n Weave a two color table mat using a dowel loom for self-expression.\n Display and appreciate own and others work.\nKEY INQUIRIES QUESTION\n How do we weave a table mat in two colors using a dowel loom?\n How do we create different patterns on a mat when weaving?\n How do we compress weft thread when weaving?\n What do you like about the mat you have woven?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Communication and collaboration\n Self-efficacy\n Problem solving skills\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Respect\n Sharing\n responsibility\nSelf-esteem\nSelf-awareness\nHealth and hygiene\nLearning Resources\nWoven articles, dowel loom, sticks, banana fiber, reeds, rope ,\nStrings, strips of fabric, cutting tools, ICT devices, sticks.\nKLB Visionary art and craft Grade 2 Teachers Guide pg. 90-95\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pair/groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT\nStep 1: Display to learner’s sample of woven items from teacher’s collection and or digital media.\nStep 2: Guide learners to identify and name materials for making a dowel loom and for weaving.\nStep 3: Demonstrate and guide learners how to weave a mat in two colours using a dowel loom.\nStep 4: Guide learners to weave and do finishing.\nSUMMARY\nWeaving is the process of interlacing or intertwining two sets of thread known as warp and weft.\nCONCLUSION\nLearners to display their work and talk about others work.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to make decorative woven table mats that can be used at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "b7bafb8863a44416", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES (2).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 2443, "text": "ner Download Safety education a href https drive google com uc export\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Waste disposal in the classroom\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\na. Identify types of waste found in the classroom.\nb. Identify containers used for putting waste in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat waste is found in the classroom?\nWhere do we put waste in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of different kinds of dusts bins.\nColours, crayons or felt pens.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 57-59.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the picture of a classroom shown on page 57 of the learner’s book.\nStep 2: Learners to study the picture and answer the questions provided on the same page of the learner’s\nworkbook. The class in not clean.\nStep 3: Allow the learners to identify the waste they can see in the classroom and what waste is and how it\nshould be disposed of.\nStep 4: Learners to look around their own class and see whether the waste is disposed of properly.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nUnhealthy classroom attracts disease causing germs and pests.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate appropriate waste disposal.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Waste disposal in the classroom\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to Practice waste collection and paper disposal of the waste found in\nthe classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhere do we put waste in the classroom?\nHow do we dispose waste collected in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of different kinds of dusts bins.\nColours, crayons or felt pens.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 57-59.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the picture of a clean classroom shown on page 58 of the learner’s book.\nStep 2: Learners to compare the two classrooms and let them identify the differences.\nStep 3: Learners to discuss which classroom they would like to learn in and why.\nStep 4: Learners to identify waste in their classroom and throw it on the dust bin\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nUnhealthy classroom attracts disease causing germs and pests.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate appropriate waste disposal.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Waste disposal in the classroom\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\na. Mention the importance of waste disposal in the classroom.\nb. Appreciate the importance of proper disposal of waste in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we dispose waste collected in the classroom?\nWhy should we dispose waste collected in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of different kinds of dusts bins.\nColours, crayons or felt pens.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 57-59.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the picture of a clean classroom shown on page 58 of the learner’s book.\nStep 2: Learners to compare the two classrooms and let them identify the differences.\nStep 3: Learners to discuss which classroom they would like to learn in and why.\nStep 4: Learners to identify waste in their classroom and throw it on the dust bin\nStep 5: learners are provided with space to draw a classroom on page 59 of the learner’s workbook.\nStep 6: Learners to discuss with their classmates and answer questions in the learner’s workbook.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nUnhealthy classroom attracts disease causing germs and pests.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate appropriate waste disposal.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify common accidents in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat are the common accidents in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the picture of Sarah tripping over a school bag on learner’s book page 60.\nStep 2: Learners to discuss what they think caused Sara to fall and what else in their environment can cause\none to trip and fall\nStep 3: Learners to identify the common causes of accidents in their classroom\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify common accidents in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat are the common accidents in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the two pictures of two classrooms shown on learner’s book page 61.\nStep 2: Learners to discuss and identify the dangers they see in both pictures.\nStep 3: Learners to identify the common causes of accidents in the two classrooms.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to Name the causes of common accidents in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat are the causes of common accidents in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the two pictures of two classrooms shown on learner’s book page 61.\nStep 2: Learners to discuss and identify the dangers they see in both pictures.\nStep 3: Learners to identify the causes of common accidents in the two classrooms.\nStep 4: Guide learners to realize that a clean and organized classroom has less dangers than an untidy one.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\na. Name the causes of common accidents in the classroom.\nb. Name ways of preventing common accidents in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat are the causes of common accidents in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES.\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on the two pictures of two classrooms shown on learner’s book page 61.\nStep 2: Learners to discuss and identify the dangers they see in both pictures.\nStep 3: Learners to identify the causes of common accidents in the two classrooms.\nStep 4: Guide learners to realize that a clean and organized classroom has less dangers than an untidy one.\nStep 5: Guide learners through the picture on learner’s book page 62 of Bora having bumps on his head. The\nbump was caused in the classroom.\nStep 6: Guide learners to what could have caused the bump and how it could be prevented. Discuss with them\nthe first treatments also.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to Demonstrate First Aid for common accidents in the classroom.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat is the simple First Aid for common accidents in the classroom?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners through the picture on learner’s book page 62 of Bora having bumps on his head. The\nbump was caused in the classroom.\nStep 2: Guide learners to what could have caused the bump and how it could be prevented. Discuss with them\nthe first treatments also.\nStep 3: Guide learners through the picture of Flora on learner’s workbook page 63.\nStep 4: Guide learners to discuss what could have caused the prick and how it can be avoided. The first Aid\ntreatment is also provided on page 63 of the learner’s workbook.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: SAFETY EDUCATION\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Common accidents and Basic First Aid\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\na. Demonstrate First Aid for common accidents in the classroom.\nb. Demonstrate responsibility in preventing accidents in the classroom\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat is the simple First Aid for common accidents in the classroom?\nWhat is the simple First Aid for common accidents in the classroom?\nHow can these dangers in the classroom be prevented?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Citizenship\n Health issues\n Environmental issues\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of bumps, pricks and grazes.\nComputer and internet.\nFirst aid box.\nVideo showing First Aid Treatment.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 60-66.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners through the picture on learner’s book page 64 of Fara having graze. The bump was\ncaused in the classroom.\nStep 2: Guide learners to what could have caused the graze and how it could be prevented.\nStep 3: Guide learners to discuss what could have caused the prick and how it can be avoided. The first Aid\ntreatment is also provided on page 65 of the learner’s workbook.\nStep 4: Guide the learners to role play the First Aid treatment for Bumps, pricks and grazes.\nStep 5: Guide the learners to remove all the hazards in their classroom that can cause bumps, pricks and grazes.\nStep 6: Guide the learners in understanding that first aid is first help given to someone before they get medical\nattention.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nIt is important to keep classroom safe and free from accidents.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to appreciate and discuss the hazards that can cause accidents in the classroom.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "4dead8d007b496b8", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/LESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES (3).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 2224, "text": "ner Download Personal hygiene a href https drive google com uc export\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify the items used for personal cleanliness.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when taking bath.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to list and write them in their exercise books.\nStep 3: Learners to discuss how these items are used.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleanliness in important for health\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items.\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify the items used for personal cleanliness.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when taking bath.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in a discussion to show why these items should not be shared.\nStep 3: Guide learners to colour some personal items on page 29 of learner’s workbook.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleanliness in important for health\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items (toothbrush)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\na. Identify the items used for personal cleanliness\nb. Give reasons why we should not share personal items.\nc. State the procedure used when cleaning items for personal use.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nHow do we clean personal items?\nWhy should we not share personal items?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when brushing their teeth.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in the steps provided in the learner’s workbook on how to clean a toothbrush.\nStep 3: Give learners an opportunity to clean a toothbrush if possible.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleaning personal items in important\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\n. Learners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items (toothbrush)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:\n1. Identify the items used for personal cleanliness\n2. Give reasons why we should not share personal items.\n3. State the procedure used when cleaning items for personal use.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nHow do we clean personal items?\nWhy should we not share personal items?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when brushing their teeth.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in the steps provided in the learner’s workbook on how to clean a toothbrush.\nStep 3: Give learners an opportunity to clean a toothbrush if possible.\nStep 4: Lead learners in a discussion why they should not share a toothbrush i.e. in order to avoid spread\nof disease/infections.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleaning personal items in important\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSUBSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items (comb)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\n1. Identify the items used for personal cleanliness.\n2. Give reasons why we should not share personal items.\n3. State the procedure used when cleaning items for personal use.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nHow do we clean personal items?\nWhy should we not share personal items?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when combing their hair.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in the steps provided in the learner’s workbook on how to clean a comb.\nStep 3: Give learners an opportunity to clean a comb if possible.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleaning personal items in important\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN HYGIENE AND NUTRITION ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERSONAL HYGIENE\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Use and care of personal items (face towel)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to:-\n1. Identify the items used for personal cleanliness.\n2. Give reasons why we should not share personal items.\n3. State the procedure used when cleaning items for personal use.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat items do we use for personal cleanliness?\nHow do we clean personal items?\nWhy should we not share personal items?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Critical thinking\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Responsibility\n Love\n Respect\n Health issues\n Life skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPicture of personal items.\nMaterials for cleaning personal items: water, soap, brush.\nComputers/tablets.\nFocus on Hygiene and nutrition Grade 2 learners’ workbook pg. 29-35.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION.\nReview the previous lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the items they use when cleaning their face.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in the steps provided in the learner’s workbook on how to clean a face towel.\nStep 3: Give learners an opportunity to clean a face towel if possible.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nCleaning personal items in important\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to promote personal hygiene among family members and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON