s in a kneeling position?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Citizenship\n Self-efficacy\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Creativity and imagination\n Communication and collaboration\n Critical thinking and problem solving\n Responsibility\n Sharing\n patience\nCitizenship\nLife skills\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nF", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "e00113745dbb3577", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/MUSIC ACTIVITIES LESSON PLAN.docx", "title": "MUSIC ACTIVITIES LESSON PLAN", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 6698, "text": "n Plans Performing a href https drive google com uc export download\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES LESSON PLAN\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTwo\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: SONGS: Types of Songs\nSpecific lesson learning outcome\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Identify different types of songs performed for different purposes to enhance comprehension.\nb) Sing a variety of age appropriate songs in unison paying attention to accuracy in pitch and rhythm for\nenjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  During which activities do you sing?\n2. What do the songs that you sing talk about?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Recordings of different types of songs.\n ICT devices.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 2-4.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in groups and individually.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song of their choice.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Play a recording of different types of songs for listening and guide learners to talk about the occasions\nwhen the songs are performed and the message they contain.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to identify the different types of songs according to purpose/message, style of\nperformance and occasion when they are performed.e.g lullabies, story songs, and sacred songs.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to sing relevant familiar songs to illustrate the type of song and the message\ncontained in the song. E.g. Lullaby “u kira mwana”.\nStep 4: Learners to act out and role play the content of songs as they sing.e.g “crossing the road”.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by learners asking questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nClose the lesson through singing age appropriate songs and paying attention to pitch and rhythm.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to listen to different types of songs and identify the various types of songs.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTwo\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Two part Rounds\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to sing a two-part round for enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Why is singing done in groups?\n2. What do you feel when you sing in a group?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Video recordings.\n Song scores with lyrics.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 4-6.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide learners to sing a familiar song to prepare them for singing lesson.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to sing a two- part round e.g. “Mama Mbe Tsimbindi” following the steps below\nSing the song in unison as a whole class\nGroup the class into two\nEach group to sing in unison to ascertain that they know the song well\nConduct the round, where one group starts the song and the other one begins to sing after the\nfirst phrase\nStep 2: Discuss with the learners the need to practice regularly and keeping time for rehearsals.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to sing a second song e.g. “We come from many places” as a round\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by asking learners to sing a 2-part rounds drawn from the local culture.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to listen to melodic flow of rounds so as to lean to sing the round song by imitation.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to sing round in two parts in well-coordinated groups.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTwo\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Kenya National Anthem\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to sing verse 1 and 2 of the Kenya National Anthem in\nKiswahili and English with proper etiquette to enhance patriotism, cohesion and peaceful co-existence.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  When is Kenya national anthem performed?\n2. What do we learn in verse 1 of the Kenya national anthem?\n3. What do we learn in verse 2 of the Kenya national anthem?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Music score and words of them\n Pictures of some occasions when the anthem is performed\n ICT devices and recordings of the anthem\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 6-9\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide the learners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Conduct a discussion on the meaning of the words of the national anthem in relation to patriotism,\ncohesion and peaceful co-existence in 2nd verse\nStep 2: Guide the learners to recite the words of the 2nd verse in English and or Kiswahili\nStep 3: Teach the 2nd verse phrase by phrase and guide the learners to sing verse two as a whole.\nStep 4: Guide the learners to sing verse one and two in English and o Kiswahili while following etiquette by\nstanding at attention, removing unofficial caps and showing respect for the country.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by asking the learners to sing the national anthem in groups.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide the learners to sing verse 2 o the anthem in both Kiswahili and English\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to listen to melody and text of Kenya national anthem and to imitate by singing.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTwo\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Elements of music and vocal techniques\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Sing different songs while observing the elements of music as a basis of musicianship.\nb) Use different vocal techniques while singing for effective communication.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  What elements of music would you apply in singing?\n2. What vocal techniques would you use for effective communication?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Music scores and lyrics.\n ICT devices.\n Recorded music.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 9-10.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work I groups and individually.\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide the learners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the basic elements of music to be effected in singing; pitch, tempo,\ndynamics and rhythm.\nStep 2: Guide them to sing familiar songs, talk about where the elements can be applied and with what effect.\nStep 3: Learners to sing the songs in pairs, in groups and as a class applying the elements e.g. Miti\nyayumbayumba\nStep 4: Use the same song or other suitable songs to guide learners to identify vocal techniques to be used in\nsinging for effective communication.\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by asking learners to name other techniques used in singing for effective\ncommunication.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nUse suitable songs for learners to develop vocal techniques.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to listen and to identify and to apply various elements of music and vocal techniques while singing\neither at school or at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Expression of ideas, feelings and emotions through singing\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Express ideas, feelings and emotions through singing for self-expression and effective communication.\nb) Sing familiar songs with body movements for self-expression.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  What songs do you like to sing?\n2. What do you learn from the song?\n3. What do you feel when you sing them?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT equipment.\n Music scores.\n Song book.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 11-13.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners will work in groups and individually.\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide the learners to sing familiar songs to draw the attention to the messages in the songs.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Play a recording of selected presentations for learners to listen to or watch and guide learners to\nidentify the expressions of ideas, feelings and emotions.\nStep 2: Guide them to know how expression of ideas, feelings and emotions are actualized through\nunderstanding the meaning of words, use of facial expressions, gestures and body movements.\nStep 3: Guide them to sing short familiar songs, expressing ideas, feelings and emotions.\nStep 4: Guide the learners to identify vocal techniques e.g. positioning of the mouth, body posture, breathing\ntechnique and tone colour.e.g singing of “we should be clean”\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by guiding the learners to sing the song “We should be clean”\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGuide the learners to sing other familiar songs with facial expressions, gestures and body movements\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nThe learner to listen and respond by singing songs at home and school while observing expression of ideas,\nfeelings and emotions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Solo and Choral performances\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to perform familiar songs individually and as a solo\nperformance or in a choral group for communication and enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  Which songs would you enjoy singing alone?\n2. Which song do you enjoy singing with others in a group?\n3. Why do you sing in a group?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Song book\n ICT devices\n Relevant music scores and lyrics\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 13-14\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the musical elements and vocal techniques and apply them as they\nperform the songs\nStep 2: Guide the learners to sing solo presentations and discuss with them the presentations.\nStep 3: Guide them to make choral presentations and discuss the performances and the need to practice\nregularly.\nSUMMARY\nGuide the learners on the importance of keeping time for rehearsal and striving to achieve targets, for them to\ngrow and become responsible performers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing any solo or choral song.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to listen to live or recorded presentations and appreciate singing styles.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSUBSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Songs from diverse cultures\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to appreciate and enjoy singing songs from diverse cultures.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  What do you like about songs sung in your language?\n2. What do you like about song sung in other languages?\n3. Why should we sing song in other languages?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n road safety awareness\n coping with emotions\n community service learning and parental\ninvolvement\n turn taking\n learner support programmes\n health education\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Recorded songs performance on ICT devices.\n ICT devices.\n Song books.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 14-15.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners will work in groups or individually.\nINTRODUCTION\nAsk the learners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to appreciate and understand the messages in the songs from different cultures and\nthat they are similar in quality of melodies and in message.\nStep 2: Play recorded songs from diverse cultural background for the learners to listen to and to talk about the\nvaluable aspects of the cultural messages.\nStep 3: Guide the learner to sing some of the songs listened to and others sung in class e.g. lullabies.\nStep 4: Organize learners to sing the songs in pairs, small groups and then as the whole class.\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by holding a discussion on the importance of preserving the original form of cultural\nsongs.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing songs from other cultures that they are familiar with.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to listen to different types of songs and the occasions when the songs are performed.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Singing Games\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Perform various singing games from the local culture for enjoyment.\nb) Perform various singing games from other cultures for enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  Which songs do children like singing?\n2. Which songs do children sing while playing?\n3. What do children imitate while singing such songs?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Audio/visual materials.\n Costumes.\n Singing games song book.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 19-22.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual or group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in watching live and recorded singing games from the local culture and other\ncultures.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to perform singing games from the locality individually and in groups.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to perform singing games from other cultures individually and in groups.\nStep 4: Guide the learners to identify values and pertinent issues addressed in the singing games.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to record singing games using simple recording devices such as smartphones\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nAssign the learners a project of performing a singing game and be recorded by the parent or guardian.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Aspects of singing games\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to perform singing games from local and other cultures with\nappropriate application of aspects of:-\na) Singing for enjoyment.\nb) Movement for enjoyment.\nc) Props for enjoyment, and.\nd) Costumes for enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  How do you tell a good singer?\n2. How do you tell a good dancer?\n3. How are performers of singing games dressed?\n4. What materials or things are used while performing singing games?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Audio-visual devices\n Costumes.\n Relevant props.\n Singing game book.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 22-28.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual and group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing and dance to a familiar song .\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the following aspects of singing: Rhythm, pitch, Diction, Soloist, Breath\ncontrol, Tonality.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to identify the following aspects of movement. Dancing in rhythm, making patterns,\nbeing agile, and coordination of movement, choreography and patterns, good use of space.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to identify different props in different singing games such as ropes, dolls, stones etc.\nStep 4: Learners to appreciate the role of costumes by identifying costumes used by different performers\nlocally and from other cultures.\nSUMMARY:\nLearners to apply the aspects learnt while performing different appropriate singing games from their local and\nother cultures e.g. “Mwana Mukhana”\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to singing familiar songs accompanied by the learnt aspects.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to performance various singing games in assistance with their friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Safety precautions in singing games\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to take precautions during performance of singing games:-\na) From local cultures for own safety.\nb) From other cultures for others, safety.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1.  What injuries do children get while playing?\n2. What injuries can a child get while performing singing games?\n3. What should children do to avoid injuries while performing singing games?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Audio-visual devices.\n Costumes.\n Improvised props.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 28-31.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual and group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing familiar song from the local or other cultures.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the causes of injuries among others. E.g. accidents, falling.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to identify the injuries children get while playing e.g. fractures, bruises and cuts.\nStep 3: Guide the learners in discussing safety precautions.\nStep 4: Learners to observe video performances as they appreciate safety in singing games.\nSUMMARY:\nLet learners perform appropriate singing games from other cultures while observing safety precautions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nAsk learners questions on the safety precautions to be observed in singing and performing.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nProvide further opportunities of practice for learners to perfect their skills.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Etiquette in singing games\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Perform singing game following games etiquette for own and other’s respect.\nb) Share available resources during performance of singing games for equity and integrity.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. How do people respect one another?\n2. When do you become unhappy with yourself and friends while performing a sing game?\n3. What play things/apparatus are shared while performing a singing game?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Recorded videos.\n Relevant props e.g. ropes, tins.\n Costumes.\n Improvised props.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 31-34.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual and group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing and dance to a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners through the inquiry questions to show how performers show respect to each other as\nyou acknowledge such responses.\nStep 2: Guide the learners to identify the play apparatus commonly used in singing games such as ropes, bin\nbags, sticks, toys, shakers and costumes.\nStep 3: Guide the learners to discuss the importance of sharing equipment while performing singing games.\nSUMMARY:\nAsk the learner questions on the observance of etiquette in singing games.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing a familiar while observing etiquette.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice at home and in school as they are observed by parents and friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Singing games from diverse cultures\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Appreciate singing games from diverse cultures.\nb) Perform various singing games from the diverse cultures for enjoyment\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. What role do you like taking during play?\n2. What do you like in the performance of singing games from other areas?\n3. What do you learn from the performance of singing games from other areas?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Critical thinking and\nproblem solving\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Love\n Integrity\n patriotism\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Relevant items in the environment.\n Props.\n Costumes.\n Singing game book.\n Worksheets.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 34-37.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual and group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing and dance a familiar song for vocal exercise and physical warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Manipulate simple electronic equipment as they watch and listen to singing games from diverse\ncultures.\nStep 2: Leaners to work in groups and individually to collect props and costumes.\nStep 3: Learners to perform singing games from diverse cultures as a whole, in groups and individually\nSUMMARY\nInvolve learners in recording the performances with their tablets.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nAsk learners questions on various singing games from diverse cultures.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nInvolve learners in recording performances at home and performing before their parents.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Musical instruments: Wind instruments\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Name wind instruments used in music making for familiarization.\nb) Identify different wind instruments used to accompany song and dance for familiarization.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Which instruments are played while singing or dancing?\n2. How are the instruments played?\n3. Which instruments are played by blowing?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Social justice\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n health\n financial literacy\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Wind instruments from the locality.\n Wind instruments from other cultures.\n Pictures of wind instruments.\n Appropriate recorded sounds of different wind instruments.\n ICT devices.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 41-42.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nIndividual and group work.\nINTRODUCTION\nIntroduce wind instruments as those played by blowing by displaying charts.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to identify the difference from one instrument to the other.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in naming the win instruments identified.\nStep 3: Guide learners on how to draw, sketch, join dots or match the instruments with the corresponding\ninstrument.\nSUMMARY\nAsk learners questions about names of wind instruments they know.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to listen and identify wind instruments sounded live or recorded\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearner to practice and improvise wind instruments to produce sound at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Playing of wind instruments\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Demonstrate the skills of playing a wind instrument for skill development.\nb) Observe safety and care of the instruments for maintenance and storage.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. How are wind instruments played?\n2. How are wind instruments held while playing?\n3. Which parts of your body can you use while playing a wind instrument?\n4. How important are these parts of the body in playing of wind instruments?\n5. What should be done to keep wind instruments safe?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Social justice\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n health\n financial literacy\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Recorded videos.\n Relevant props e.g. ropes, tins.\n Costumes.\n Improvised props.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 42-45.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work and individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nDo warm up activities for breath control and fingering.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners through the playing skills that include holding, placement of the lips, fingering and\nbreath control.\nStep 2: Guide learners to play instruments as they observe appropriate skills.\nStep 3: Learners to play in groups, in pairs and individually as they make observations about the skills\ndisplayed.\nStep4: Guide the learners on safety on storage of the instruments.\nStep 5: Guide the learners on the appropriate ICT device to be used for further learning.\nSUMMARY\nAsk the learners to name some of the wind instruments they know.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to listen and identify wind instruments sounded live or recorded.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to visit cultural centers to help them improve their skills by interacting with specialists.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Improvising wind instruments\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Improvise accompaniments to songs and dances using improvised wind instruments for enjoyment.\nb) Observe own and others; safety in improvising and playing wind instruments?\nc) Appreciate own improvised musical instruments and accompaniments and those of others.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) What materials are used to make wind instruments?\n2) How are improvised wind instruments made?\n3) What should be done to keep wind instruments safe?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Social justice\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n health\n financial literacy\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Pictures of wind instruments.\n Appropriate reusable materials found within the environment such as pipes and straws.\n Reeds.\n Cutting tools.\n Bottles.\n Water.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 45-47.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work and individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nPlay the improvise wind instruments to accompany familiar songs.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to collect and use locally available materials to come up with simple wind\ninstruments.\nStep 2: Learners to work in groups in collecting and gathering the materials.\nStep 3: Learners to observe hygienic standards while handling re-usable materials.\nSUMMARY\nObserve which materials the learners will use to improvise wind instruments\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to listen and identify wind instruments sounded live or recorded\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to visit cultural centers to help them improve their skills by interacting with specialists.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Playing virtual instruments\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Play virtual musical instruments to enhance playing skills.\nb) Play virtual musical instruments for digital literacy.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) What are musical instruments used for?\n2) Why do we play instruments on computer?\n3) How do you lay the instruments on computer or phone?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Respect\n Responsibility\n Unity\n Social justice\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\n health\n financial literacy\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Video and audio recordings of musical instruments covered.\n ICT devices.\n Pictures of instruments.\n ICT devices installed with relevant virtual programmes for musical instruments.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 47-48.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work and individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to watch video clips of musical performances with instruments.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to play real instruments.\nStep 2: Guide the learners on playing the virtual instruments. Software should be learner friendly.\nStep 3: Give learners an opportunity to learn from each other and appreciate each other’s work and let them\npay attention to how the instruments look like, how they are played and the unique sound they produce.\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by asking the learners questions on which virtual instruments they can download from\nthe internet.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play selected virtual instruments to perfect skills.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to play virtual instruments at home and in school by listening and imitation.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dance: Body movements (waving, greetings, digging, sweeping)\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Use body movements that are part of daily experience in a variety of ways in dance for enjoyment for\ndance.\nb) Use dance to express feelings, emotions and ideas for self-expression.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) What are some of the body movements you make in your daily life?\n2) Which of the body movements can you use in a dance?\n3) Which parts of the body do you use when dancing?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n integrity\n love\n patriotism\n unity\n environmental awareness\n animal awareness\n safety\n health education\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Relevant songs.\n Recordings of dances.\n Resource persons.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 52-54.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work and individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a son accompanied by body movements.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to watch live or recorded dances in order to observe various body part movements\nused.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in identifying movements of body parts that are involved in a dance.\nStep 3: Discuss with the learners the need to use body movements that enhance respect and moral values.\nStep 4: Learners to pair or group and prepare own dances for presentation in class. Guide the learners in\nadjudicating group presentations using adjudicator sheets in their work sheets.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by asking learners questions on day to day movements they make.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nConclude the lesson by learners singing any song of choice accompanied by body movements.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice both at school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dance: Body movements (Locomotor and non-Locomotor)\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Apply Locomotor and non-Locomotor/axial movements creatively in own dance for enjoyment.\nb) Observe own and others’ safety during dance performance.\nc) Practice etiquette during dance performance for integrity.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) Which parts of the body are used when dancing?\n2) How do you feel when you dance as you make moves?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n integrity\n love\n patriotism\n unity\n environmental awareness\n animal awareness\n safety\n health education\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Musical instruments\n Resource persons.\n Recorded dances.\n Costumes.\n Relevant songs.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 54-55.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup and individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing familiar dance songs for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in watching live or recorded dances which shows the various movements of body\nparts.\nStep 2: Assist the learners to plan the formation of dance and to combine singing, dance styles and making\nformations as they dance\nStep 3: Learners to pair and group to perform their own dances.\nStep 4: Discuss with the learners the need to use body movements to enhance respect and moral values.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by asking questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an assignment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to present best dances during school function or community cultural festival.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Dance performance\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to observe basic elements of dance during performance.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Which parts of the body are used when dancing?\n2. What is the importance of spacing while dancing?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n integrity\n love\n patriotism\n unity\n environmental awareness\n animal awareness\n safety\n health education\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Instrumental/song accompaniment\n Recorded video clips.\n Costumes.\n Resource person.\n Work sheets.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 55-57.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work or individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to do warm-up activities like stretching legs, arms, neck and toes.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in performing familiar dances.\nStep 2: Learners to identify the basic elements of dance e.g. space, time, energy, and relationship during\nperformance.\nStep 3: Guide the learners in presenting a familiar dance as they observe the basic elements of dance.\nStep 4: Learners to prepare group dances for a presentation in class.\nSUMMARY\nMake summary by asking questions on dance performance.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to adjudicate class dance presentation using adjudication sheets in their work sheets.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to identify one dance that is to be performed in a school function.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Traditional dances\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to perform appropriate traditional dances from their locality for\nsocial cohesion and enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) Which are some of the dances performed in other localities?\n2) What do you like most about the dances?\n3) How do you like the use of the instruments and costumes used?\n4) What is different when you compare dances in your locality and those from other localities?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n integrity\n love\n patriotism\n unity\n environmental awareness\n animal awareness\n safety\n health education\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Instrumental/song accompaniment.\n Recorded video clips.\n Costumes.\n Resource person.\n Adjudication sheets.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 57-58.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup work/individual.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to do warm up activities such as stretching various parts of the body by jogging, breathing and vocal\nexercise.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in performing dances as they observe the basic elements of dance as well as use of\naccompaniments and costumes.\nStep 2: Learners to prepare for group dances for presentation in class, while others adjudicate.\nStep 3: Discuss and guide learners to collect and perform Kenyan dances as a way of enhancing patriotism.\nSUMMARY:\nSummarize the lesson by asking questions on traditional dances to see what learners know about them.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing a traditional dance of their choice with accompaniments and costumes.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to identify dances to be performed during a school function.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSUBSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: PERFORMING\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Traditional dances (from diverse cultures)\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Appreciate and enjoy performing traditional dances from diverse cultures for social cohesion and\npreservation of culture.\nb) Use digital devices for learning, taking pictures, recording videos of traditional dances for presentation and\nenjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) Which are some of the dances performed in other localities?\n2) What do you like most about dances?\n3) How do you like the use of the instruments and costumes?\n4) What is the difference between dances in your locality and those from other localities\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES (PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Learning to learn\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n integrity\n love\n patriotism\n unity\n environmental awareness\n animal awareness\n safety\n health education\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT devices.\n Instrumental.\n Recorded video clips.\n Costumes.\n Resource person.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 58-60.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION.\nLearners to do warm up activities as guided by the teacher e.g. stretching.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to watch live or recorded dances as they observe the application of basic elements, use of\ncostumes and accompaniments.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in performing and presenting familiar dances as they work in groups.\nStep 3: Learners to prepare group dances for adjudication in class.\nSUMMARY:\nAsk learners questions about traditional dances they know about i.e. when they are performed.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to perform any traditional dance of choice while observing basic elements and costumes.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to identify dances for performance in a school function.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "50a5a04431a5ac80", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 2/Music Lesson Plan — Grade 2.docx", "title": "Music Lesson Plan — Grade 2", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 2", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 2106, "text": "Download Creating or Composing a href https drive google com uc export\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Rhythm awareness\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Identify animals and objects that make rhythmic patterns for rhythm awareness.\nb) Mimic animals and objects that make rhythmic patterns for rhythm awareness.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Which items make the same beat many times?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Items that produce constant beat like a clock.\n Simple recording equipment such as smart phones.\n Relevant songs.\n Relevant recorded rhythms.\n Flash cards.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 64-67.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to identify animals that make rhythmic patterns.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners on safety precautions of the instruments to be used.\nStep 2: Guide learners on key words and expressions the learner will interact with in the lesson.\nStep 3: Learners to undergo activities of identifying animals that make rhythmic patterns.\nStep 4: Display pictures of an elephant and clock and show sounds they make.\nSUMMARY:\nMake summary by asking learners to name animals and objects that make rhythms naturally.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice making rhythmic patterns naturally either by clapping or as directed by the teacher.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice making rhythmic patterns both at home and at school.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Rhythm patterns\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to create simple rhythmic patterns with short and ling\nsounds and silences (rests) using body percussion for enjoyment.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Which animals in the environment make repeated rhythmic sounds?\n2. Which machines/objects make repetitive rhythmic patterns?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Simple recording equipment such as smart phones.\n Flash cards.\n Resource person.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 67-69.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to clap/vocalize rhythms of syllables in names.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners through activities of clapping syllables in names.e.g tea, mango.\nStep 2: Guide the learners through activities of clapping and vocalizing syllables in names.\nStep 3: Learners to create simple rhythmic patterns that contain sounds of the same value.\nStep 4: Learners to create simple rhythmic patterns that contain sounds of different value.\nSUMMARY:\nLearners to identify the lengths of sounds in words.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to practice by clapping\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to the activities of creating and imitating rhythms both at school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Improvising rhythms\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to improvise rhythmic accompaniment to simple and\nfamiliar songs to enhance composition skills.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1) Which animals in the environment make repetitive rhythmic patterns?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Simple recording equipment such as smart phone.\n Relevant songs.\n Flash cards.\n Resource person.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 69-72.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to improvise rhythms using body percussion.\nStep 2: Learners to use body percussions to improvise rhythms for accompaniment to familiar songs.\nSUMMARY\nObserve and listen to improvised body rhythms by learners.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nGive an out of class assignment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice at home a project that involves the parents/guardians.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Rhythmic accompaniment to songs\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) create rhythmic accompaniment to familiar songs as a basis for composition.\nb) Record own created rhythms on digital devises to make improvements after listening and for digital\nliteracy.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. Which animals in the environment make repeated rhythmic sounds?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n Critical thinking\n citizenship\n Respect\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n education for sustainable development\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n Simple recording equipment such as smart phones.\n Relevant songs.\n Flash cards.\n Resource person.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 72-74.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners through activities of creating own rhythms by tapping/clapping to accompany\nsongs and stories.\nStep 2: Guide the learners in accompanying songs with the created rhythmic accompaniments.\nStep 3: Learners to record the created rhythms using smartphones.\nStep 4: Discuss with learners the outcomes.\nSUMMARY\nLearners to listen to improvised body rhythms and how to accompany the familiar songs\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing a familiar song with accompanying of improvised rhythms.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nAn out of class activity that will ensure participation of learner’s parent/guardian.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Melody (identifying melodic variation)\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify melodic variations in familiar tunes in\npreparation for creating own songs and for enjoyments.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\na) Which variations can be introduced in a song to make it more interesting?\nb) Which songs are performed loudly or softly?\nc) Why are songs performed slowly?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n love\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n A repertoire of simple tunes.\n ICT devices and equipment.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 77-78.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide the learners to sing familiar songs for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners to sing familiar tunes to set the right mood for the lesson.\nStep 2: Learners to sing and hum the tune with some variations and let them repeat or imitate the new\ntunes with the variations.e.g. The song “twende sokoni”\nStep 3: Learners to identify sections of melodic variations through singing them out.\nSUMMARY:\nAsk learners questions on the identification of melodic variations.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing tunes with tempo and dynamic variations\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to record their own performances that have sections of melodic repetitions at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Improvising simple melodic variations\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Improvise simple melodic variations to familiar tunes as foundation for composition and for\nenjoyment.\nb) Appreciate own and others composition.\nc) Record and play back own variations to familiar songs for preservation, enjoyment and for digital\nliteracy.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\na) How can familiar tune be made more interesting?\nb) Why do we make variations to tunes?\nc) How can we make variations to known tunes?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n love\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n A repertoire of simple tunes.\n ICT devices such as mobile phones, laptops, tablets.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 78-79\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Choose TWO or two of the learners to sing a familiar song and guide them to make variations as\nothers sing back.\nStep 2: Learners to work in groups by choosing a group leader to lead by singing with some variations as\nthe rest in the group imitates.\nStep 3: Give directions on recording of the melodies created for each group using ICT devices.\nStep 4: learners to play back the melodies for appreciation and preservation.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by posing questions on what the learners have learnt.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nObserve and listen to learners as they create melodic variations in well-known tunes.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice recording of tunes before their parents at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Improvising lyrics/texts to tunes\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Improvise new words to known tunes for creativity and enjoyment.\nb) Appreciate own and others’ composition.\nc) Record and play back their recordings for enjoyment and digital literacy.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. How can familiar tune be made more interesting?\n2. Why do we make variations to tunes?\n3. How can we make variations to known tunes?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n love\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n a repertoire of simple tunes.\n ICT devices such as mobile phones, learning laptops, tablets.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 80-82.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a familiar song for introduction.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Learners to sing familiar song then set other words to the same song.\nStep 2: Learners to prepare to repeat or imitate the changes. E.g. “Are you sleeping Brother John”\nStep 3: Learners to record new creations with the new words using ICT devices.\nStep 4: Learners to play back own recordings and others’ for appreciation.\nSUMMARY:\nListen to learners creations of new words to familiar tunes.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to continue creating new lyrics to tunes and record them.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to carry out an out of class activity as directed by the teacher.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nMUSIC ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTWO\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: CREATING/COMPOSING MUSIC\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Making presentations of own compositions\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to:-\na) Appreciate own and others’ compositions.\nb) Use digital devices to play own variations to familiar songs for enjoyment and digital literacy.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\n1. How did you make changes to the tunes?\n2. How did you make the new words to put into the known tunes?\nCORE COMPETENCIES VALUES PERTINENT CONTEMPORARY ISSUES\n(PCIs)\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Creativity and imagination\n Digital literacy\n Self-efficacy\n love\n Responsibility\n unity\n life skills\n decision making skills\n financial literacy\n citizenship\nLEARNING RESOURCES:\n ICT equipment.\n Relevant tunes.\n KLB Visionary Music Activities Grade 2 Teachers guide pg. 82-83.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nGroup/individual work.\nINTRODUCTION\nGuide learners to sing a familiar song for warm up.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Organize learners in preparation for presentation.\nStep 2: Let each group to make their presentations giving a short review of how they made the project\nand the familiar song they have used to come up with the new songs.\nStep 3: After learners presentation, discuss with the learners and the rest of the class give feedback.\nSUMMARY\nSummarize the lesson by learners asking questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to sing familiar song of own choice for perfection.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice adjudication both at school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS", "ocrText": "", "imageOcrText": "", "imageCount": 0}
{"id": "f8e49da77c7cd950", "path": "lesson-plans/Grade 3/LESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES (2).docx", "title": "LESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES", "area": "lesson-plans", "level": "Grade 3", "format": "docx", "pages": null, "words": 12121, "text": "Mathematics activities all term 2 a href https drive google com uc exp\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Number concept (Position names)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to use ordinal number names to identify position from 11 to\n15\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you identify positions?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFlash cards.\nFelt pens.\nPicture cards.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.101.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 88-89.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to form groups and engage in a running competition.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Prepare 15 numbers cards with ordinal number names and arrange them on the Teacher’s desk\nrandomly, face down.\nStep 2: Ask learners to pick a card from the teacher’s desk one at a time until they are finished. Ask the\nlearners to say the positions of the learners picking the cards. E.g. the first learner picked…\nStep 3: Draw a table and ask learners to fill in their names against the position number they have picked\nfrom the first, second, third up to fifteenth.\nStep 4: Guide learners to read the ordinal number names first, second, third up to fifteenth while\nemphasizing positions eleventh, twelfth, thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth.\nStep 5: Using the example in the learners book page 99, guide the learners to use ordinal number names\nto identify the positions.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on reading numbers\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving position.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice listening to stories of the elderly at home on how they used to count their possessions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Number concept (Position 1st to 15th)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to use ordinal number names to identify position from 11 to\n15.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you identify positions?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFlash cards\nFelt pens\nPicture cards\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.102\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 90\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to form groups and engage in a running competition.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Prepare 15 numbers cards showing different types of cutlery found at home. Also prepare 15\nnumbers cards with ordinal number names on one side and corresponding number symbols on the other.\nGuide learners in pairs or in groups to arrange the 15 picture cards from the right of their desks.\nStep 2: Learners to place number cards with ordinal number names first, second, third up to fifteenth on\nthe picture cards.\nStep 3: Guide learners to turn over the number cards with ordinal number symbols 1st, 2nd, 3rd, up to 15th\nand read the symbols while placing emphasis on 11th, 12th, 13th, 14th, and 15th.\nStep 4: Using the activity in the learners book page 100, guide the learners to use ordinal number\nsymbols 1st, 2nd, 3rd, up to 15th.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on reading numbers\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving position.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice listening to stories of the elderly at home on how they used to count their possessions.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Whole Numbers (Counting in fives)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to count in fives forward and backwards from 1 to 1000\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you count numbers?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nNumber cards.\nNumber line.\nNumbers charts.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg. 103.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 91-92.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to count objects in fives forward.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to count objects in fives both forward and backward.\nStep 2: Learners in groups to place number cards on the groups to show number of objects in the group.\nStep 2: Using the example in the learner’s book page 101, guide learners to count in fives forward and\nbackwards from 1 to 1000\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in community functions as a way of promoting\nlearning outside the school.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Place Value\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify place value up to tens.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you identify place value?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBundles of sticks.\nAbacus.\nPlace value tins.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.104-105.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 92-93.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to collect sticks in bundles for use in identifying place value.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to represent various numbers using bundles of sticks.\nStep 2: Ask the learners in pairs or in groups to identify ones and tens from the bundles of sticks and\nloose ones.\nStep 3: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to represent various numbers using place value apparatus and\nidentify ones and tens.\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 102, guide learners to identify place value up to\ntens.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Place Value\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be to identify place value up to hundreds.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you identify place value?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBundles of sticks.\nAbacus.\nPlace value tins.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.106-107.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 93-94.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to collect sticks in bundles for use in identifying place value.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to represent various numbers using bundles of sticks and\nloose ones.\nStep 2: Ask the learners in pairs or in groups to identify ones, tens and hundreds from the bundles of\nsticks and loose ones.\nStep 3: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to represent various numbers using place value apparatus and\nidentify ones, tens and hundreds.\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 104, guide learners to identify place value of ones,\ntens and hundred.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Reading numbers in symbols\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to read numbers in symbols 1 to 100 .\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nWhat do we use to represent groups of objects?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nFlash cards.\nNumber chart.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.108.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 95\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a song involving numbers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to read the numbers in turns using the number chart.\nStep 2: Ask the learners in pairs or groups to read numbers from number cards or flash cards.\nStep 3: Using the example in the learners book pg. 106, guide the learners in reading whole numbers 1 to\n100.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Reading numbers in words\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to read numbers in symbols 1 to 100 in words.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we read numbers in words?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nNumber charts with number symbols and number names.\nFlash cards with number symbols.\nFlash card with number name.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.109.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 95-96.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a song involving numbers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to read the numbers 1 to 100 in words.\nStep 2: Ask the learners in pairs or groups to match number words with number symbols (1 to 100).\nStep 3: Using the example in the learners book pg. 107, guide the learners in reading whole numbers 1 to\n100.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Writing Numbers in words\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to read numbers 1 to 100 in words.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow can numbers in symbols be written in words?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nNumber charts with number symbols and number names.\nFlash cards.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.110.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 96-97.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a song involving numbers.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to read the numbers 1 to 100 in words.\nStep 2: In pairs or groups, a learner displays flash cards with number symbols while others write the\nnumber name.\nStep 3: Using the example in the learners book pg. 108, guide the learners in writing whole numbers 1 to\n100 in words.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on counting numbers.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving counting.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school and at home….\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Number patterns\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to work out missing numbers in number patterns\ninvolving numbers 1 to 500.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we identify missing numbers in number pattern?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nNumber cards.\nNumber charts.\nNumber line.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.111.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 97-98.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a song involving numbers using skipping rope.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in pairs or groups to arrange numbers using number cards in an increasing\norder 1 to 500 (from any point to a maximum of 10 numbers)\nStep 2: Guide the learners to create a pattern by removing a card(s) and skipping one or more to the next.\nLearners to write the resulting pattern from activity two and share with other groups.\nStep 3: Guide learners in pairs or groups to arrange numbers using number cards in a decreasing order\n500 to 1 (from any point to a maximum of 10 numbers)\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 109, guide the learners in identifying the missing\nnumbers in a number pattern.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on number patterns.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving Numbers\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Number patterns\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to work out missing numbers in number patterns\ninvolving numbers 1 to 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we identify missing numbers in number pattern?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nNumber cards.\nNumber charts.\nNumber line.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.111.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 98-99.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to sing a song involving numbers using skipping rope.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide the learners in pairs or groups to arrange numbers using number cards in an increasing\norder 1 to 1000 (from any point to a maximum of 10 numbers)\nStep 2: Guide the learners to create a pattern by removing a card(s) and skipping one or more to the next.\nLearners to write the resulting pattern from activity two and share with other groups.\nStep 3: Guide learners in pairs or groups to arrange numbers using number cards in a decreasing order\n1000 to 1 (from any point to a maximum of 10 numbers)\nStep 4: Guide learners to create a pattern by removing a card(s) and skipping one or more to the next.\nLearners to write the resulting pattern and share with other groups.\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 110, guide the learners to work out missing\nnumbers in a number pattern involving numbers 1 to 1000\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on number patterns.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving Numbers.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to arrange chairs on tables in rows and columns in the school and at home.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Fractions (Eighth as part of a whole)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify eighth as part of a whole.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we represent a eighth as part of a whole?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nManilla cutouts\nPair of scissors\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.113\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 100-101\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare cut outs of different objects\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to cut rectangular cut outs. Discuss the safety precautions\nwhen handling sharp objects.\nStep 2: Learners in pairs or groups to discuss how to get 1/8 of a cutout.\nStep 3: Guide learners to fold the rectangular cutouts into 8 equal parts and identify one part as a 1/8 of\nthe whole\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 111, guide learners to identify 1/8 as part of a\nwhole.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on fractions: eighth as part of whole.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving fractions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice sharing of items in eighths and quarters at home and at school with their friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Fractions (Comparing a Quarter and an Eighth)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to compare a quarter and an eighth as part of a whole.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we compare ¼ and 1/8 of a whole?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nManilla cutouts.\nPair of scissors.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.114.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 100-101.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare cut outs of different objects.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in pairs or in groups to cut circular rectangular cut outs. Discuss the safety\nprecautions when handling sharp objects.\nStep 2: Learners in pairs or groups to discuss how to get a quarter and an eighth of a whole using the cut-\nouts prepared in activity one (1)\nStep 3: Guide learners to fold circular and rectangular cutouts into 4 and 8 equal parts and identify a\nquarter and an eighth as part of a whole\nStep 4: Using the example in the learner’s book page 112, guide learners to identify and compare a\nquarter and an eighth as part of a whole.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on fractions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving fractions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice sharing of items in eighths and quarters at home and at school with their friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Fractions (quarter as part of a group)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify quarter as part of a group.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we represent a quarter of a group?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBottle tops.\nSticks.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.115-116.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 102-103.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING.\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare cut outs of different objects.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate the sharing of bottle tops in four equal groups guide learners to identify one group\nas a quarter of the whole group.\nStep 2: Group learners in fours. Give each group a number of counters and let the share the bottle tops\nequally.\nStep 3: Guide learners to identify that one group of the four groups represent a quarter of the group.\nStep 4: Using activities in the learner’s book page 113, guide learners to identify quarter as part of the\ngroup.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on fractions: quarter as part of group.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving fractions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice sharing of items in halves and quarters at home and at school with their friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Fractions Eighth as part of a group)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to identify eighth as part of a group.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we represent an eighth of a group?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBottle tops.\nSticks.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.117.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 103-104.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare cut outs of different objects.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Demonstrate the sharing of bottle tops in eight equal groups guide learners to identify one group\nas an eighth of the whole group.\nStep 2: Group learners in eighths. Give each group a number of counters and let the share the bottle tops\nequally.\nStep 3: Guide learners to identify that one group of the eight groups represent an eighth of the group\nStep 4: Using activities in the learner’s book page 115, guide learners to identify an eighth as part of a\ngroup.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on fractions: Eighth as part of group.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving fractions.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice sharing of items in halves and quarters at home and at school with their friends.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a 3-digit number to a 2- digit number)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a 3 digit number to a 2-digit number without\nregrouping vertically with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPlace value tins.\nPlace value chart.\nAbacus.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg. 118-119.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg105.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value apparatus to add a 3-digit number to a 1-\ndigit number without regrouping vertically and horizontally. Discuss the safety precautions when\nhandling the resource materials.\nStep 2: Guide learners in groups to discuss how to add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number without\nregrouping vertically with a sum not exceeding 1000\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 116, guide the learners to add a 3-digit number to a\n2-digit number without regrouping.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on addition by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving addition\nGive an assessment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice working out total number of different items in their homes.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a 3-digit number to a 2- digit number)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a 3 digit number to a 2-digit number without\nregrouping horizontally with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPlace value tins.\nPlace value chart.\nAbacus.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg. 120.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 106.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value apparatus to add a 3-digit number to a 2-\ndigit number without regrouping. Discuss the safety precautions when handling the resource materials.\nStep 2: Guide learners in groups to discuss how to add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number without\nregrouping horizontally with a sum not exceeding 1000\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 118, guide the learners to add a 3-digit number to a\n2-digit number horizontally without regrouping.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on addition by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving addition\nGive an assessment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice working out total number of different items in their homes.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a 3-digit number to a 2- digit number)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a 3 digit number to a 2-digit number with\nregrouping from ones vertically and horizontally with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number involving regrouping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPlace value tins.\nPlace value chart.\nAbacus.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg. 121-122.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 107.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value apparatus to add a 3-digit number to a 2-\ndigit number with regrouping from ones with sum not exceeding 1000. Discuss the safety precautions\nwhen handling the resource materials.\nStep 2: Guide learners in groups to discuss how to add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number with\nregrouping from ones vertically and horizontally with a sum not exceeding 1000\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 119, guide the learners to add a 3-digit number to a\n2-digit number vertically and horizontally with regrouping with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on addition by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving addition\nGive an assessment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice working out total number of different items in their homes.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a 3-digit number to a 2- digit number)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a 3 digit number to a 2-digit number with\nregrouping from tens vertically with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do you add numbers involving regrouping?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPlace value tins.\nPlace value chart.\nAbacus.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg. 123-124.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 108.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value apparatus to add a 3-digit number to a 2-\ndigit number with regrouping from tens with sum not exceeding 1000. Discuss the safety precautions\nwhen handling the resource materials.\nStep 2: Guide learners in groups to discuss how to add a 3-digit number to a 2-digit number with\nregrouping from tens vertically and horizontally with a sum not exceeding 1000\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 121, guide the learners to add a 3-digit number to a\n2-digit number with regrouping vertically with a sum not exceeding 1000.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on addition by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving addition\nGive an assessment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice working out total number of different items in their homes.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSUBSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a 3-single digit numbers)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a 3 single digit numbers with sum not exceeding\n20.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we add 3-single numbers?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nBottle tops.\nSticks.\nGrains.\nNumber line.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.125.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 109.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value chart to add a 3-single digit numbers\nwith sum not exceeding 20 using concrete objects. Discuss the safety precautions when handling the\nresources and materials.\nStep 2: Ask the learners in pairs to discuss how to add a 3-single digit numbers\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 123, guide the learners to add a 3-single digit\nnumbers with sum not exceeding 20.\nSUMMARY\nReview the lesson on addition by asking learners questions.\nCONCLUSION (Assessment of Learning)\nLearners to play digital games involving addition\nGive an assessment.\nEXTENSION OF ACTIVITIES\nLearners to practice working out total number of different items in their homes.\nREFLECTION ON THE LESSON/SELF-REMARKS\nLESSON PLAN MATHEMATICS ACTIVITIES\nWeek:      Lesson:\nSCHOOL GRADE DATE TIME ROLL\nTHREE\nSTRAND/THEME/TOPIC: NUMBERS\nSUBSTRAND/SUB-THEME/SUB-TOPIC: Addition (Adding a two 3- digit numbers)\nSpecific lesson learning outcome.\nBy the end of the lesson, the learner should be able to add a two 3-digit numbers vertically and horizontally\nwithout regrouping with sum not exceeding 1000.\nKEY INQUIRY QUESTION (s)\nHow do we add two 3-digit numbers?\nCore competencies Values PCIs\n Learning to learn\n Communication and\ncollaboration\n Imagination and\ncreativity\n Problem solving\n Unity\n Respect\n Patriotism\n responsibility\nSelf-awareness\nSelf-esteem\nLEARNING RESOURCES\nPlace value tins.\nPlace value chart.\nAbacus.\nMathematics pupil’s book 3 pg.126-127.\nMathematics teachers guide grade 3 pg. 109-110.\nORGANIZATION OF LEARNING\nLearners to work in pairs or groups.\nINTRODUCTION\nLearners to prepare count total number of desks in their class.\nLESSON DEVELOPMENT (Assessment as learning)\nStep 1: Guide learners in in pairs or groups in using place value apparatus to add a two 3-digit numbers\nwithout   regrouping with sum not exceeding 1000. Discuss the safety precautions when handling the\nresources and materials.\nStep 2: learners in pairs to discuss how to add a two 3-digit numbers vertically and horizontally without\nregrouping with sum not exceeding 1000.\nStep 3: Using the example in the learner’s book page 124, guide the learners to add a two 3- digit\nnumbers vertically and horizontally without regrouping with sum not exceeding 1000.\nSUMMARY\nReview the les